/* Boss lore: background for every boss (7 Nov 2026). New lore awaiting John's approval as canon. */
window.VHBOSSLORE={"sb_win_race_b":{"n":"The Wind-Rival","bg":"The Wind-Rival has won the Skyway race eleven years running by hiding a stolen bit of storm in his saddle. He is not fast; he is in debt, because stolen storm ages the holder and he has been paying in years he did not have. Underneath the cheating he is thirty and looks fifty. Beating him may be the kindest thing anyone has done for him.","ln":["Dov: Careful. It ages the one who holds it."]},"sb_gra_vein_b":{"n":"The Seam-Wight","bg":"The Seam-Wight hums a lullaby through the lower seam, the song the first miner sang to keep her children asleep underground. Gravem holds weight; the mountain held the song. Three miners walked into it and lay down. The Wight is the lullaby's guardian, and it only wants people to rest in the stone with it.","ln":["Miners' work-chant (later): Hush now, the mountain is holding."]},"sb_gra_stone_b":{"n":"The Stone Mourner","bg":"The Stone Mourner is a statue of a long-dead Senator in the Gallery of the Dead that has begun to move. Stonehold carves its dead, and this one wanted to be asked whether he was proud of what he built. Nobody asked when he was alive, because Senators are not asked; they are obeyed.","ln":["Gallery plaque: Served for life. Asked nothing. Answered nothing."]},"sc_win_storm_b":{"n":"The Storm He Made","bg":"The Storm He Made is Stormcaller Aeld's lifetime of castings come back to collect. He is thirty-four and looks eighty-one, because every storm took a week. He wants to settle the account in one sitting, and the storm's eye is where he does it. The player stands guard while he pays.","ln":["Aeld: Every casting steals a week. I have been very rich in storms."]},"alpha":{"n":"Blackroot Alpha","bg":"The Blackroot Alpha was the last of the dire hounds the old Ashford warden kept to guard the root cellars, left to run wild when the warden died and nobody claimed the leash. The pack learned that barley sacks and farm dogs are easier than deer. It is not cruel; it is fed, and that is all it has ever been taught to want. Tobb blames himself for not taking the hounds in when the warden was buried, and that is why he asks so carefully.","ln":["Ashford rumour: Old Hesk's hounds never forgot the cellar door. Only the man who opened it."]},"blackroot alpha":{"n":"Blackroot Alpha","bg":"The Blackroot Alpha was the last of the dire hounds the old Ashford warden kept to guard the root cellars, left to run wild when the warden died and nobody claimed the leash. The pack learned that barley sacks and farm dogs are easier than deer. It is not cruel; it is fed, and that is all it has ever been taught to want. Tobb blames himself for not taking the hounds in when the warden was buried, and that is why he asks so carefully.","ln":["Ashford rumour: Old Hesk's hounds never forgot the cellar door. Only the man who opened it."]},"wellwarden":{"n":"The Well-Warden","bg":"The Well-Warden is what the Old Well grew around a lullaby. Pip's father sang into it every night of the hard winter, because the echo carried his voice to the children's loft, and when he died the well kept singing without him. The Warden is the shape of that song refusing to end: soft, cold and very protective of the one thing dropped into it. It gives the lantern up warm because, in its way, it has been holding it the whole time.","ln":["Inscription on the well-cap: Sing down, and it sings back.","Pip: Mama says it is just a well. Mama does not go out at night."]},"the well-warden":{"n":"The Well-Warden","bg":"The Well-Warden is what the Old Well grew around a lullaby. Pip's father sang into it every night of the hard winter, because the echo carried his voice to the children's loft, and when he died the well kept singing without him. The Warden is the shape of that song refusing to end: soft, cold and very protective of the one thing dropped into it. It gives the lantern up warm because, in its way, it has been holding it the whole time.","ln":["Inscription on the well-cap: Sing down, and it sings back.","Pip: Mama says it is just a well. Mama does not go out at night."]},"scout-captain rolf":{"n":"Scout-Captain Rolf","bg":"Rolf was a Dominion surveyor before he was a scout-captain, and his maps are better than his swordwork. Metalcrown sent him south a season before the harvest to mark every village in grey: where the grain is, where the young men are, which roads a column could use. He believes he is preventing a war by knowing exactly how it would go. He is the first face the player puts on the Iron Dominion, and he is very ordinary.","ln":["Wen: Forty years I counted smoke. Never once did it count me back."]},"scoutcap":{"n":"Scout-Captain Rolf","bg":"Rolf was a Dominion surveyor before he was a scout-captain, and his maps are better than his swordwork. Metalcrown sent him south a season before the harvest to mark every village in grey: where the grain is, where the young men are, which roads a column could use. He believes he is preventing a war by knowing exactly how it would go. He is the first face the player puts on the Iron Dominion, and he is very ordinary.","ln":["Wen: Forty years I counted smoke. Never once did it count me back."]},"the cold anvil":{"n":"The Cold Anvil","bg":"The Cold Anvil is not a beast but a forge-guard, a lump of slag the slag-imps raised around Garrick's grandfather's anvil-heart to keep it for themselves. Slag-imps are drawn to iron that will not cool, and the heart has hummed under Ashford since the hard winter sealed the cellar. The Anvil wakes when anyone reaches for it, the way coals flare when the door opens. Smiths in the district still knock twice on the cellar door before going down.","ln":["Smith's rhyme: Iron that never cools never forgets the hand.","Garrick: Grandad said it hummed his wedding song. He never told us which one."]},"anvil":{"n":"The Cold Anvil","bg":"The Cold Anvil is not a beast but a forge-guard, a lump of slag the slag-imps raised around Garrick's grandfather's anvil-heart to keep it for themselves. Slag-imps are drawn to iron that will not cool, and the heart has hummed under Ashford since the hard winter sealed the cellar. The Anvil wakes when anyone reaches for it, the way coals flare when the door opens. Smiths in the district still knock twice on the cellar door before going down.","ln":["Smith's rhyme: Iron that never cools never forgets the hand.","Garrick: Grandad said it hummed his wedding song. He never told us which one."]},"cutter brask":{"n":"Cutter Brask","bg":"Cutter Brask was a Hearthmount bladesmith who lost his temper at the Hearth Court, broke a judge's jaw and was exiled to the slag fields. Embercraft took more of his restraint each year he used it, until he could not keep a workshop, only a camp. The Dominion's grey coin found him there and asked for blades; he takes smiths instead of making them now. Davin's refusal unsettles him because it is the thing he once was.","ln":["Orla: Brask taught me to quench a blade. He said, never in anger. He should have listened."]},"brask":{"n":"Cutter Brask","bg":"Cutter Brask was a Hearthmount bladesmith who lost his temper at the Hearth Court, broke a judge's jaw and was exiled to the slag fields. Embercraft took more of his restraint each year he used it, until he could not keep a workshop, only a camp. The Dominion's grey coin found him there and asked for blades; he takes smiths instead of making them now. Davin's refusal unsettles him because it is the thing he once was.","ln":["Orla: Brask taught me to quench a blade. He said, never in anger. He should have listened."]},"keeper":{"n":"The Shrine Keeper","bg":"The Shrine Keeper is the guardian the first Flameborne set over the stone where they burned a feeling into rock, so that the Realm would never forget what it felt like to refuse. Nobody agrees which feeling it was. The Keeper only lets the ember-heart go to someone who says aloud whose it should be, because Embercraft always costs the one who holds it. It tests the hand, not the blade.","ln":["Shrine inscription: Burn what you are given. Keep what you choose.","Veyra: You will stand straighter. You will not know why. That is the price."]},"the shrine keeper":{"n":"The Shrine Keeper","bg":"The Shrine Keeper is the guardian the first Flameborne set over the stone where they burned a feeling into rock, so that the Realm would never forget what it felt like to refuse. Nobody agrees which feeling it was. The Keeper only lets the ember-heart go to someone who says aloud whose it should be, because Embercraft always costs the one who holds it. It tests the hand, not the blade.","ln":["Shrine inscription: Burn what you are given. Keep what you choose.","Veyra: You will stand straighter. You will not know why. That is the price."]},"the blotmother":{"n":"The Blotmother","bg":"The Blotmother rises in the ink wells under the annex, where ink for the Lantern Codex is drawn. Codex ink takes on the shape of whatever it records; when a thing is struck out, the ink that wrote it drains back to the wells. The Blotmother is a century of struck-out things pooling together until they remember how to stand. She wants to be written back in.","ln":["Brother Ione: Nothing should be forgotten twice.","Well-keeper's chalk: DO NOT DRAW AFTER DARK."]},"blot":{"n":"The Blotmother","bg":"The Blotmother rises in the ink wells under the annex, where ink for the Lantern Codex is drawn. Codex ink takes on the shape of whatever it records; when a thing is struck out, the ink that wrote it drains back to the wells. The Blotmother is a century of struck-out things pooling together until they remember how to stand. She wants to be written back in.","ln":["Brother Ione: Nothing should be forgotten twice.","Well-keeper's chalk: DO NOT DRAW AFTER DARK."]},"captain orrick":{"n":"Captain Orrick","bg":"Captain Orrick commanded the Solbrand house guard three generations ago and was buried with the Halberd of First Light at his side. When a grave-robber took it, Orrick rose to follow, and the Solbrand dead rose with him, because Radiance does not let a broken oath lie. He is not a monster; he is a soldier pursuing stolen property at walking pace. Under the First Light even the dead must account for what they guard.","ln":["Solbrand epitaph: Truth without action is cowardice.","Sentinel Cael: The dead are walking after it. I find that I respect them."]},"orrick":{"n":"Captain Orrick","bg":"Captain Orrick commanded the Solbrand house guard three generations ago and was buried with the Halberd of First Light at his side. When a grave-robber took it, Orrick rose to follow, and the Solbrand dead rose with him, because Radiance does not let a broken oath lie. He is not a monster; he is a soldier pursuing stolen property at walking pace. Under the First Light even the dead must account for what they guard.","ln":["Solbrand epitaph: Truth without action is cowardice.","Sentinel Cael: The dead are walking after it. I find that I respect them."]},"judge thane":{"n":"Judge Thane","bg":"Judge Thane presides over the cells under the Hall of Judgment, where those who cannot yet say the truth are held until they can. He is not sadistic. He is a Lightforge judge who has used Radiance for thirty years, and each verdict burned away a little of his own past until the law is all he remembers. Holding a six-year-old for a lie about soup is, to him, simply procedure.","ln":["Mender Sova: He asked the boy to swear by the First Light. The boy asked what that was."]},"judge":{"n":"Judge Thane","bg":"Judge Thane presides over the cells under the Hall of Judgment, where those who cannot yet say the truth are held until they can. He is not sadistic. He is a Lightforge judge who has used Radiance for thirty years, and each verdict burned away a little of his own past until the law is all he remembers. Holding a six-year-old for a lie about soup is, to him, simply procedure.","ln":["Mender Sova: He asked the boy to swear by the First Light. The boy asked what that was."]},"maren":{"n":"Glass-cutter Tesk","bg":"Glass-cutter Tesk was a Citadel vault clerk who learned that Metalcrown buyers would pay a fortune for memory-glass that could not lie. She cut panels one at a time and told herself the memories were already given away. Spiritveil draws the glass; Lightforge keeps it as testimony; neither ever asked the people inside. Tesk only asked the price.","ln":["Quartermaster Ostrand: Richest trade in the world. I have never once been able to look at it."]},"glass-cutter tesk":{"n":"Glass-cutter Tesk","bg":"Glass-cutter Tesk was a Citadel vault clerk who learned that Metalcrown buyers would pay a fortune for memory-glass that could not lie. She cut panels one at a time and told herself the memories were already given away. Spiritveil draws the glass; Lightforge keeps it as testimony; neither ever asked the people inside. Tesk only asked the price.","ln":["Quartermaster Ostrand: Richest trade in the world. I have never once been able to look at it."]},"echo of captain ysen":{"n":"Echo of Captain Ysen","bg":"Captain Ysen led a Lightforge company in a border skirmish outside Meridian whose ending nobody remembers, because every survivor paid for the victory with their past. The field kept what they could not. Ysen and his soldiers are echoes fighting the last hour on loop, waiting for an order that never came. Wren goes to tell him it is over because remembering is Spiritveil's work.","ln":["Wren: Remember a grief and it lives. That is the job."]},"ysen":{"n":"Echo of Captain Ysen","bg":"Captain Ysen led a Lightforge company in a border skirmish outside Meridian whose ending nobody remembers, because every survivor paid for the victory with their past. The field kept what they could not. Ysen and his soldiers are echoes fighting the last hour on loop, waiting for an order that never came. Wren goes to tell him it is over because remembering is Spiritveil's work.","ln":["Wren: Remember a grief and it lives. That is the job."]},"enforcer valda":{"n":"Enforcer Valda","bg":"Enforcer Valda is a Lightforge Edict officer sent to hold the Skybridge relay so Stormreach cannot talk to its own towers. She is good at her job, and she believes the Edict is the only thing standing between the Realms and the Void. She was a courier once, Stormreach-born, and joined Lightforge after a storm-casting aged her mother to death. She does not regulate storms for the Edict. She regulates them for her mother.","ln":["Orrin Gale: I could ride in and die gloriously. I would rather you did it slowly and well."]},"valda":{"n":"Enforcer Valda","bg":"Enforcer Valda is a Lightforge Edict officer sent to hold the Skybridge relay so Stormreach cannot talk to its own towers. She is good at her job, and she believes the Edict is the only thing standing between the Realms and the Void. She was a courier once, Stormreach-born, and joined Lightforge after a storm-casting aged her mother to death. She does not regulate storms for the Edict. She regulates them for her mother.","ln":["Orrin Gale: I could ride in and die gloriously. I would rather you did it slowly and well."]},"drake":{"n":"Skyrazor Drake","bg":"The Skyrazor Drake is the mother of a ridge-top roost of lightning drakes that never came down until the Edict began regulating storms. Drakes feed on the charge in Stormreach weather; tame the weather and they starve. She is an animal following her food into the Spire. Aeris kills her because there is no other way, and does not pretend otherwise.","ln":["Aeris: A thing like that deserved a better death than the Edict gave it."]},"skyrazor drake":{"n":"Skyrazor Drake","bg":"The Skyrazor Drake is the mother of a ridge-top roost of lightning drakes that never came down until the Edict began regulating storms. Drakes feed on the charge in Stormreach weather; tame the weather and they starve. She is an animal following her food into the Spire. Aeris kills her because there is no other way, and does not pretend otherwise.","ln":["Aeris: A thing like that deserved a better death than the Edict gave it."]},"riot-king garrow":{"n":"Riot-King Garrow","bg":"Riot-King Garrow is a Flameborne dock-worker who stood on a cart when the Edict was read in the Spire's Flameborne quarter and did not get down. Embercraft burns away self-control, and he has been burning since the first stone. He holds half the district hostage because he cannot think of another way to be heard. He is not a monster; he is out of restraint.","ln":["Brann Ashe: Flameborne does not do quiet. Quiet is how they win."]},"garrow":{"n":"Riot-King Garrow","bg":"Riot-King Garrow is a Flameborne dock-worker who stood on a cart when the Edict was read in the Spire's Flameborne quarter and did not get down. Embercraft burns away self-control, and he has been burning since the first stone. He holds half the district hostage because he cannot think of another way to be heard. He is not a monster; he is out of restraint.","ln":["Brann Ashe: Flameborne does not do quiet. Quiet is how they win."]},"hurl":{"n":"Edict Marshal Hurl","bg":"Edict Marshal Hurl leads the Lightforge column sent to force Stonehold's compliance through the Refusal Pass. He is a career officer who has never lost a column and does not intend to start against a Realm that refuses to move. He believes in the Edict the way a mason believes in a plumb line. Stonehold's refusal is, to him, a structural fault.","ln":["Envoy Dorrin: Stonehold does not run. We hold."]},"edict marshal hurl":{"n":"Edict Marshal Hurl","bg":"Edict Marshal Hurl leads the Lightforge column sent to force Stonehold's compliance through the Refusal Pass. He is a career officer who has never lost a column and does not intend to start against a Realm that refuses to move. He believes in the Edict the way a mason believes in a plumb line. Stonehold's refusal is, to him, a structural fault.","ln":["Envoy Dorrin: Stonehold does not run. We hold."]},"gravem's shadow":{"n":"Gravem's Shadow","bg":"The Sentinel's Shadow (in game: Gravem's Shadow) is what the Silent Sentinel left in the Hall of Seals when he buried the Aetherguard's last seal a hundred years ago. Gravem, the Aether of gravity and endurance, gave his shadow weight, and it has been standing on the seal ever since. He cannot speak, so it cannot either. It yields only to someone who carries the seal back to him.","ln":["Carved by the Sentinel: LATE."]},"gshadow":{"n":"Gravem's Shadow","bg":"The Sentinel's Shadow (in game: Gravem's Shadow) is what the Silent Sentinel left in the Hall of Seals when he buried the Aetherguard's last seal a hundred years ago. Gravem, the Aether of gravity and endurance, gave his shadow weight, and it has been standing on the seal ever since. He cannot speak, so it cannot either. It yields only to someone who carries the seal back to him.","ln":["Carved by the Sentinel: LATE."]},"the runaway colossus":{"n":"The Runaway Colossus","bg":"The Runaway Colossus was a wall-golem cut in the Stonehold quarries and set to march to the border wall. It heard the Radiant Edict read aloud on the quarry road and decided that no one regulates stone. Golems are made with a single sentence carved inside them; this one's sentence was 'hold the line', and it has chosen which line. It walks towards Meridian at the pace of a mountain.","ln":["Quarry saying: Carve the sentence small. Stone takes everything literally."]},"colossus":{"n":"The Runaway Colossus","bg":"The Runaway Colossus was a wall-golem cut in the Stonehold quarries and set to march to the border wall. It heard the Radiant Edict read aloud on the quarry road and decided that no one regulates stone. Golems are made with a single sentence carved inside them; this one's sentence was 'hold the line', and it has chosen which line. It walks towards Meridian at the pace of a mountain.","ln":["Quarry saying: Carve the sentence small. Stone takes everything literally."]},"kess":{"n":"Outrider Captain Kess","bg":"Outrider Captain Kess escorts sealed Dominion wagons north on the Iron Road. She was a Stonehold carter's daughter who took Dominion pay when the Senate's road tolls ruined her family. She knows every rut on the road because she drove it as a child. She does not ask what is in the wagons, on principle.","ln":["Sera Ironvow: I held it with my shield and your hands. I do not usually say that."]},"outrider captain kess":{"n":"Outrider Captain Kess","bg":"Outrider Captain Kess escorts sealed Dominion wagons north on the Iron Road. She was a Stonehold carter's daughter who took Dominion pay when the Senate's road tolls ruined her family. She knows every rut on the road because she drove it as a child. She does not ask what is in the wagons, on principle.","ln":["Sera Ironvow: I held it with my shield and your hands. I do not usually say that."]},"slag-warden tull":{"n":"Slag-Warden Tull","bg":"Slag-Warden Tull runs the Dominion's black prison in the furnace pits north of the Keep, where forty Flameborne sit collared in iron. The collars suppress Embercraft, which keeps them quiet in a way that is worse than shouting. Tull is a Metalcrown merit-officer who has never struck a prisoner. He simply tightens the collars on schedule.","ln":["Tamsin Ashveil: Do you know what it means for a Flameborne to be quiet that long?"]},"tull":{"n":"Slag-Warden Tull","bg":"Slag-Warden Tull runs the Dominion's black prison in the furnace pits north of the Keep, where forty Flameborne sit collared in iron. The collars suppress Embercraft, which keeps them quiet in a way that is worse than shouting. Tull is a Metalcrown merit-officer who has never struck a prisoner. He simply tightens the collars on schedule.","ln":["Tamsin Ashveil: Do you know what it means for a Flameborne to be quiet that long?"]},"marek":{"n":"Interrogator Marek","bg":"Interrogator Marek runs the black site where the Dominion keeps Noctis, the Iron Shade, Metalcrown's first engineered machine-wraith, whom children's stories warn of. Marek's job is to find out what Noctis is for, and in eleven years he has not managed it. He fears Noctis and will not kill him. He has started asking him questions that are not in the file.","ln":["Quartermaster Ves: The file said only: the Iron Shade. Noctis."]},"interrogator marek":{"n":"Interrogator Marek","bg":"Interrogator Marek runs the black site where the Dominion keeps Noctis, the Iron Shade, Metalcrown's first engineered machine-wraith, whom children's stories warn of. Marek's job is to find out what Noctis is for, and in eleven years he has not managed it. He fears Noctis and will not kill him. He has started asking him questions that are not in the file.","ln":["Quartermaster Ves: The file said only: the Iron Shade. Noctis."]},"mirrorwraith":{"n":"The Mirror-Wraith","bg":"The Mirror-Wraith lives in the frozen mirrors of an ice cave under the Keep, where every reflection is slightly late. It is a Chrona echo: a reflection that saw a little ahead and got stuck there. Lyriel Frostwraith foresaw the collapse and paid with feeling; the mirror remembers what she cannot. It keeps the shard because the shard shows the same future every time.","ln":["Lyriel: There is a mirror that remembers what I cannot."]},"the mirror-wraith":{"n":"The Mirror-Wraith","bg":"The Mirror-Wraith lives in the frozen mirrors of an ice cave under the Keep, where every reflection is slightly late. It is a Chrona echo: a reflection that saw a little ahead and got stuck there. Lyriel Frostwraith foresaw the collapse and paid with feeling; the mirror remembers what she cannot. It keeps the shard because the shard shows the same future every time.","ln":["Lyriel: There is a mirror that remembers what I cannot."]},"grule":{"n":"Pit Overseer Grule","bg":"Pit Overseer Grule runs the Slagmoor Pits where Stonehold miners dig spark-ore in the dark for the Dominion. He is Metalcrown, half-mechanised, and has given enough humanity to the synthetic spark that he no longer understands why people want to see the sky. He keeps exact quotas. He was a miner once.","ln":["Lord Hald: Stonehold remembers its own."]},"pit overseer grule":{"n":"Pit Overseer Grule","bg":"Pit Overseer Grule runs the Slagmoor Pits where Stonehold miners dig spark-ore in the dark for the Dominion. He is Metalcrown, half-mechanised, and has given enough humanity to the synthetic spark that he no longer understands why people want to see the sky. He keeps exact quotas. He was a miner once.","ln":["Lord Hald: Stonehold remembers its own."]},"provost gannet":{"n":"Provost Gannet","bg":"Provost Gannet commands the Pitmouth Furnaces, a clerk who became a butcher without ever raising his voice. He rose through Metalcrown's merit ranks by never failing a quota, and the furnaces that feed the reactors are his masterpiece. He considers cruelty inefficient. He considers this efficient.","ln":["Captain Rhea: I did not think I would see the chimneys dark in my lifetime."]},"provost":{"n":"Provost Gannet","bg":"Provost Gannet commands the Pitmouth Furnaces, a clerk who became a butcher without ever raising his voice. He rose through Metalcrown's merit ranks by never failing a quota, and the furnaces that feed the reactors are his masterpiece. He considers cruelty inefficient. He considers this efficient.","ln":["Captain Rhea: I did not think I would see the chimneys dark in my lifetime."]},"ashgrove":{"n":"Regent Ashgrove","bg":"Regent Ashgrove guards the Rivet Hollow Works where spark-cores are built to wake Iron Wraiths. She is a Synthesis Council regent who traded most of her humanity for a seat. The cores beat like hearts because they are modelled on one. She knows whose.","ln":["Quartermaster Dov: It still beats. Do not tell me what it sounds like."]},"regent ashgrove":{"n":"Regent Ashgrove","bg":"Regent Ashgrove guards the Rivet Hollow Works where spark-cores are built to wake Iron Wraiths. She is a Synthesis Council regent who traded most of her humanity for a seat. The cores beat like hearts because they are modelled on one. She knows whose.","ln":["Quartermaster Dov: It still beats. Do not tell me what it sounds like."]},"magistrate voss":{"n":"Magistrate Voss","bg":"Magistrate Voss drills the Dominion's newest column in Furnace Row. She is Metalcrown civil law turned soldier, a magistrate given a regiment when the Synthesis Council ran out of officers. She is good at it and does not enjoy it. When she throws down her seal, it is the first decision she has made for herself in years.","ln":["Senator Vael: The Senate serves for life. Life is long enough to be worth fighting for."]},"voss":{"n":"Magistrate Voss","bg":"Magistrate Voss drills the Dominion's newest column in Furnace Row. She is Metalcrown civil law turned soldier, a magistrate given a regiment when the Synthesis Council ran out of officers. She is good at it and does not enjoy it. When she throws down her seal, it is the first decision she has made for herself in years.","ln":["Senator Vael: The Senate serves for life. Life is long enough to be worth fighting for."]},"the iron reaper":{"n":"The Iron Reaper","bg":"The Iron Reaper is, in canon, Chief Engineer Solvane, Metalcrown's master of war-engines, and the great gun at the Iron Gate is his. He built guns for the Dominion long before the coup and has watched every one used. He holds the last gate before Iron Throne City because he designed it.","ln":["Dominion saying: The Reaper does not miss. He recalculates."]},"reaper":{"n":"The Iron Reaper","bg":"The Iron Reaper is, in canon, Chief Engineer Solvane, Metalcrown's master of war-engines, and the great gun at the Iron Gate is his. He built guns for the Dominion long before the coup and has watched every one used. He holds the last gate before Iron Throne City because he designed it.","ln":["Dominion saying: The Reaper does not miss. He recalculates."]},"scrivener":{"n":"The Blind Scrivener","bg":"The Blind Scrivener was the copyist of a reading room the Council struck from the Citadel plans. He kept copying after the door was sealed, because nobody told him to stop, and lost his sight to the dark and his past to Radiance. He copies what he is told to forget. The pages he produces are perfectly accurate and nobody is allowed to read them.","ln":["Scribe Rhen: I copy what I am told to forget. Nobody checks the copies."]},"the blind scrivener":{"n":"The Blind Scrivener","bg":"The Blind Scrivener was the copyist of a reading room the Council struck from the Citadel plans. He kept copying after the door was sealed, because nobody told him to stop, and lost his sight to the dark and his past to Radiance. He copies what he is told to forget. The pages he produces are perfectly accurate and nobody is allowed to read them.","ln":["Scribe Rhen: I copy what I am told to forget. Nobody checks the copies."]},"lanternbearer":{"n":"The Lantern-Bearer","bg":"The Lantern-Bearer carries the dead lamps of the Citadel cellar, each with a name tag. The lamps went out the night the Council stopped telling itself the truth, and Lightforge says a lie spoken in council dims every light that was lit to witness it. The Bearer will not put them down until someone tells the truth about why they went out. It is a duty, not a haunting.","ln":["Lamplighter Oda: Every lamp I light is a promise to someone who is not here yet."]},"the lantern-bearer":{"n":"The Lantern-Bearer","bg":"The Lantern-Bearer carries the dead lamps of the Citadel cellar, each with a name tag. The lamps went out the night the Council stopped telling itself the truth, and Lightforge says a lie spoken in council dims every light that was lit to witness it. The Bearer will not put them down until someone tells the truth about why they went out. It is a duty, not a haunting.","ln":["Lamplighter Oda: Every lamp I light is a promise to someone who is not here yet."]},"gale":{"n":"Gale-Mother Ysolt","bg":"Gale-Mother Ysolt was a Stormreach courier-mistress who trained couriers for forty years and aged a year for every storm she called to carry them. When she could not ride any more, she called one last storm to nest over the cliffs where her couriers trained, and became it. She takes young riders who fly too high because she taught them to. It is training, and it kills them.","ln":["Courier Fenn: I have never outrun a storm. I have only been early to it."]},"gale-mother ysolt":{"n":"Gale-Mother Ysolt","bg":"Gale-Mother Ysolt was a Stormreach courier-mistress who trained couriers for forty years and aged a year for every storm she called to carry them. When she could not ride any more, she called one last storm to nest over the cliffs where her couriers trained, and became it. She takes young riders who fly too high because she taught them to. It is training, and it kills them.","ln":["Courier Fenn: I have never outrun a storm. I have only been early to it."]},"tallow":{"n":"Tallow-King Brisk","bg":"Tallow-King Brisk runs the chandlers' cellars, melting the poor of the Spire's Flameborne quarter into candles that burn twice as hot. He was a candle-maker who found that Embercraft-touched fat burned brighter, and Embercraft took his restraint along with his conscience. Forty names are in his ledger. He signed each one in a careful hand.","ln":["Chandler Bess: Flameborne candles burn twice as hot. They also burn out twice as fast."]},"tallow-king brisk":{"n":"Tallow-King Brisk","bg":"Tallow-King Brisk runs the chandlers' cellars, melting the poor of the Spire's Flameborne quarter into candles that burn twice as hot. He was a candle-maker who found that Embercraft-touched fat burned brighter, and Embercraft took his restraint along with his conscience. Forty names are in his ledger. He signed each one in a careful hand.","ln":["Chandler Bess: Flameborne candles burn twice as hot. They also burn out twice as fast."]},"the stone-eye":{"n":"The Stone-Eye","bg":"The Stone-Eye is a single eye carved into a lower quarry ceiling by a Stonehold mason who held a collapsing gallery with Gravem until his whole body was stone, and kept watching. The masons who work beneath it turn grey from the fingers up, because his Gravem is still holding and drawing weight from whoever stands under it. He does not know the gallery was shored up years ago.","ln":["Mason Maru: I laid the keep's every stone myself. I do not remember the one that is looking at me."]},"stoneeye":{"n":"The Stone-Eye","bg":"The Stone-Eye is a single eye carved into a lower quarry ceiling by a Stonehold mason who held a collapsing gallery with Gravem until his whole body was stone, and kept watching. The masons who work beneath it turn grey from the fingers up, because his Gravem is still holding and drawing weight from whoever stands under it. He does not know the gallery was shored up years ago.","ln":["Mason Maru: I laid the keep's every stone myself. I do not remember the one that is looking at me."]},"cinderlord":{"n":"Cinder-Lord Vash","bg":"Cinder-Lord Vash is a Flameborne refugee who seized the Keep's old armoury with fire, hostages and a reason: his family was in the Slag Prison and the Senate would not vote to free them. Embercraft has burned through most of his restraint and he knows it. He walks out in the end if someone listens long enough. He is not a lord; the title is a joke the guards made.","ln":["Warden Kell: The Keep has no weak walls. That is the problem."]},"cinder-lord vash":{"n":"Cinder-Lord Vash","bg":"Cinder-Lord Vash is a Flameborne refugee who seized the Keep's old armoury with fire, hostages and a reason: his family was in the Slag Prison and the Senate would not vote to free them. Embercraft has burned through most of his restraint and he knows it. He walks out in the end if someone listens long enough. He is not a lord; the title is a joke the guards made.","ln":["Warden Kell: The Keep has no weak walls. That is the problem."]},"deepwarden":{"n":"The Deep-Warden","bg":"The Deep-Warden leads the Dominion sappers tunnelling under Highmarch. He is a Metalcrown construct, a sapper who replaced his lungs, eyes and finally his voice with machinery to work deeper. He does not sleep, eat or answer. He is the Dominion's humanity price paid in full.","ln":["Sapper Sorrel: They tell me the war is above. I am down here. It is quiet. That is what worries me."]},"the deep-warden":{"n":"The Deep-Warden","bg":"The Deep-Warden leads the Dominion sappers tunnelling under Highmarch. He is a Metalcrown construct, a sapper who replaced his lungs, eyes and finally his voice with machinery to work deeper. He does not sleep, eat or answer. He is the Dominion's humanity price paid in full.","ln":["Sapper Sorrel: They tell me the war is above. I am down here. It is quiet. That is what worries me."]},"lastsmith":{"n":"The Last Smith","bg":"The Last Smith rings an abandoned forge on the Marshes road, a man making something he knows he should not. He was a Stonehold armourer who took a Dominion commission for a blade that could cut a spark out of a body, and died before he finished. The forge keeps ringing because the commission is unfinished. Armourer Ada will not say what the blade was.","ln":["Armourer Ada: I have made ten thousand blades. I could not tell you what I wanted any of them to do."]},"the last smith":{"n":"The Last Smith","bg":"The Last Smith rings an abandoned forge on the Marshes road, a man making something he knows he should not. He was a Stonehold armourer who took a Dominion commission for a blade that could cut a spark out of a body, and died before he finished. The forge keeps ringing because the commission is unfinished. Armourer Ada will not say what the blade was.","ln":["Armourer Ada: I have made ten thousand blades. I could not tell you what I wanted any of them to do."]},"tollmaster brek":{"n":"Tollmaster Brek","bg":"Tollmaster Brek ran the drover's yard for twenty years and kept two ledgers, one for Ashford and one for whoever paid. Brek's people bought debts, and one of the debts they bought was a miller's, signed when a child was sick. Forty sacks of tithe grain were the interest. Brek believed himself an honest broker; he kept exact accounts of everything he stole.","ln":["Dessa: A cooper knows a barrel by its sound. That one rang hollow from across the yard."]},"tollmaster":{"n":"Tollmaster Brek","bg":"Tollmaster Brek ran the drover's yard for twenty years and kept two ledgers, one for Ashford and one for whoever paid. Brek's people bought debts, and one of the debts they bought was a miller's, signed when a child was sick. Forty sacks of tithe grain were the interest. Brek believed himself an honest broker; he kept exact accounts of everything he stole.","ln":["Dessa: A cooper knows a barrel by its sound. That one rang hollow from across the yard."]},"the collector":{"n":"The Collector","bg":"The Collector is Brek's last man, a quiet debt-taker who has carried Haldan's note for two years and visits the mill on the first of every month, never raising his voice. He was a farmer once, ruined the same way, and took the work because collecting is safer than owing. He does not see the note as cruelty. He sees it as weather.","ln":["Haldan: He always wiped his boots. That was the worst of it."]},"collector":{"n":"The Collector","bg":"The Collector is Brek's last man, a quiet debt-taker who has carried Haldan's note for two years and visits the mill on the first of every month, never raising his voice. He was a farmer once, ruined the same way, and took the work because collecting is safer than owing. He does not see the note as cruelty. He sees it as weather.","ln":["Haldan: He always wiped his boots. That was the worst of it."]},"wolfking":{"n":"The Wolf-King of Ashford","bg":"The Wolf-King wears the skull of a stag-wolf it killed in the hills, and the pack follows the skull more than the animal. It came down because the village split: nobody guarded the east fields together, so nobody guarded them at all. Ashford elders say a pack always smells a quarrel before a fire. It burns what it cannot carry because wolves that have learned from men have learned that, too.","ln":["Ashford proverb: Two fences against the wolf, or none.","Reeve Dessa, later: We lost a field arguing over a page."]},"the wolf-king of ashford":{"n":"The Wolf-King of Ashford","bg":"The Wolf-King wears the skull of a stag-wolf it killed in the hills, and the pack follows the skull more than the animal. It came down because the village split: nobody guarded the east fields together, so nobody guarded them at all. Ashford elders say a pack always smells a quarrel before a fire. It burns what it cannot carry because wolves that have learned from men have learned that, too.","ln":["Ashford proverb: Two fences against the wolf, or none.","Reeve Dessa, later: We lost a field arguing over a page."]},"the trial-warden":{"n":"The Trial-Warden","bg":"The Trial-Warden is the fire of the Cinder Trial given a shape, raised each turn of the moon from the brick ring when seven contenders walk. It does not judge strength. It shows each contender every feeling they own at once, which is what Embercraft does to a caster slowly, and sees who is still standing at the end. Flameborne say the Warden has never lied, only shouted.","ln":["Hearthmount saying: Stand close to the fire and it will tell you who you are, loudly."]},"trialwarden":{"n":"The Trial-Warden","bg":"The Trial-Warden is the fire of the Cinder Trial given a shape, raised each turn of the moon from the brick ring when seven contenders walk. It does not judge strength. It shows each contender every feeling they own at once, which is what Embercraft does to a caster slowly, and sees who is still standing at the end. Flameborne say the Warden has never lied, only shouted.","ln":["Hearthmount saying: Stand close to the fire and it will tell you who you are, loudly."]},"slagking":{"n":"The Slag King","bg":"The Slag King is the guardian Old Cass the Flamewright left in the First Forge forty years ago, with the method for ember-steel that burns without fire. He made it to hide the recipe from himself after it cost him his wife. It is built of every failed pour from that year and it knows the method as well as he does. Whoever takes the recipe carries the same choice he could not make well.","ln":["Cass: I made the sword. It is still hungry. Do not ask me what for."]},"the slag king":{"n":"The Slag King","bg":"The Slag King is the guardian Old Cass the Flamewright left in the First Forge forty years ago, with the method for ember-steel that burns without fire. He made it to hide the recipe from himself after it cost him his wife. It is built of every failed pour from that year and it knows the method as well as he does. Whoever takes the recipe carries the same choice he could not make well.","ln":["Cass: I made the sword. It is still hungry. Do not ask me what for."]},"the champion of the hearth":{"n":"The Champion of the Hearth","bg":"The Champion of the Hearth is chosen each generation by the old order of the Hearth Court to meet any challenger in the ash, and this one has held the ring for nine years. She has no hatred for Pyrrha. She has a hall with no roof and a Court that only respects what it sees bleed. Her Embercraft is spent almost to the bottom; she fights with very little left between her and the fire.","ln":["Court custom: The ash falls on the winner and the loser alike."]},"passionchamp":{"n":"The Champion of the Hearth","bg":"The Champion of the Hearth is chosen each generation by the old order of the Hearth Court to meet any challenger in the ash, and this one has held the ring for nine years. She has no hatred for Pyrrha. She has a hall with no roof and a Court that only respects what it sees bleed. Her Embercraft is spent almost to the bottom; she fights with very little left between her and the fire.","ln":["Court custom: The ash falls on the winner and the loser alike."]},"captain ael, hollowed":{"n":"Captain Ael, Hollowed","bg":"Captain Ael commanded Redtor Post until the Void unwrote his men one name at a time. He is gone before you arrive: blank-faced, reciting a roll of names, defending the post because that is the last order he remembers. He is the first person most players see the Void take. He was a good captain.","ln":["Sergeant Marl: I have eleven names on my roll. I could swear there were twenty-two."]},"hollowcap":{"n":"Captain Ael, Hollowed","bg":"Captain Ael commanded Redtor Post until the Void unwrote his men one name at a time. He is gone before you arrive: blank-faced, reciting a roll of names, defending the post because that is the last order he remembers. He is the first person most players see the Void take. He was a good captain.","ln":["Sergeant Marl: I have eleven names on my roll. I could swear there were twenty-two."]},"the herald":{"n":"The Herald","bg":"The Herald is the Void's memory-eater, which scouts name for what it does: it arrives before the Void. It took Redtor's roll into the old barracks and wears the names like coats. It does not die; it lets go, like a tide. In this era it only scouts; the Void behind it is stirring, not marching.","ln":["Scout Wynn: It does not hurt. That is what is wrong with it."]},"voidwarden":{"n":"The Herald","bg":"The Herald is the Void's memory-eater, which scouts name for what it does: it arrives before the Void. It took Redtor's roll into the old barracks and wears the names like coats. It does not die; it lets go, like a tide. In this era it only scouts; the Void behind it is stirring, not marching.","ln":["Scout Wynn: It does not hurt. That is what is wrong with it."]},"the stillness":{"n":"The Stillness","bg":"The Stillness is the Void's door in the high pass, a place where the world forgot to continue. Snow falls into it and does not land. It speaks one word before it falls, and the word sounds like a plea. It is an early fracture, drawn by the coming war, and it will open somewhere else.","ln":["Highmarch soldiers' saying: If your footsteps go missing, turn round."]},"hush":{"n":"The Stillness","bg":"The Stillness is the Void's door in the high pass, a place where the world forgot to continue. Snow falls into it and does not land. It speaks one word before it falls, and the word sounds like a plea. It is an early fracture, drawn by the coming war, and it will open somewhere else.","ln":["Highmarch soldiers' saying: If your footsteps go missing, turn round."]},"namethief":{"n":"The Name-Thief","bg":"The Name-Thief feeds on the memory-glass the Assize keeps as testimony and on the heads that hold the names. It is not a Void creature but something smaller and hungrier: a thing born where memories were sold, learning that names taste of the grief around them. Elder Wenna's daughter is in it. The Thief keeps the painful names longest, because those are the most filling.","ln":["Elder Wenna: It hurts, and it is mine."]},"the name-thief":{"n":"The Name-Thief","bg":"The Name-Thief feeds on the memory-glass the Assize keeps as testimony and on the heads that hold the names. It is not a Void creature but something smaller and hungrier: a thing born where memories were sold, learning that names taste of the grief around them. Elder Wenna's daughter is in it. The Thief keeps the painful names longest, because those are the most filling.","ln":["Elder Wenna: It hurts, and it is mine."]},"oathbreaker":{"n":"The Oathbreaker","bg":"The Oathbreaker haunts the Hall of Oaths, a silent hall of paper lanterns each lit by a promise somebody made. It is the shape that gathers around promises broken under the lanterns, a long-lived Spiritveil archivist's nightmare given footing. Elyndra goes in because the Record must account for promises as well as deeds. It recognises her, because she has kept every one she has made.","ln":["Lantern-hall custom: Speak your promise to the flame. The flame will speak it back."]},"the oathbreaker":{"n":"The Oathbreaker","bg":"The Oathbreaker haunts the Hall of Oaths, a silent hall of paper lanterns each lit by a promise somebody made. It is the shape that gathers around promises broken under the lanterns, a long-lived Spiritveil archivist's nightmare given footing. Elyndra goes in because the Record must account for promises as well as deeds. It recognises her, because she has kept every one she has made.","ln":["Lantern-hall custom: Speak your promise to the flame. The flame will speak it back."]},"the lantern-king":{"n":"The Lantern-King","bg":"The Lantern-King leads the nightly procession along the Lantern Road outside Lanthys Vale, a king who was never crowned, carrying every grief the living set down. Spiritveil long-livers hold their dead too long, and the road is where the excess goes. Whoever takes a grief from him keeps a stranger's last day forever. He is very tired and very polite.","ln":["Procession hymn: Carry it a mile; set it down a mile on."]},"lanternking":{"n":"The Lantern-King","bg":"The Lantern-King leads the nightly procession along the Lantern Road outside Lanthys Vale, a king who was never crowned, carrying every grief the living set down. Spiritveil long-livers hold their dead too long, and the road is where the excess goes. Whoever takes a grief from him keeps a stranger's last day forever. He is very tired and very polite.","ln":["Procession hymn: Carry it a mile; set it down a mile on."]},"stormbanner":{"n":"Stormbanner","bg":"Stormbanner is a storm-ward raised over the Storm Road by the Free Assembly to stop any courier not licensed by Stormreach. It is a banner of lightning on a pole of iron, and it has knocked nine couriers off the cliff. Kaelren means to charge it alone, because he never chooses himself. The banner tests whether he will let someone else stand with him.","ln":["Road cairn: Every courier who fell has a name here. Ride past slowly."]},"the contractor":{"n":"The Contractor","bg":"The Contractor is the man who holds Jorren's last contract, the one given to him by his Shadowdeep handler before he was old enough to refuse. The handler is dead and the contract is not. The Contractor is a debt-broker of knives who buys unfulfilled contracts from the dead and calls them in. Jorren's name is on the paper as the target.","ln":["Jorren: They made me a knife before I was a person."]},"contractor":{"n":"The Contractor","bg":"The Contractor is the man who holds Jorren's last contract, the one given to him by his Shadowdeep handler before he was old enough to refuse. The handler is dead and the contract is not. The Contractor is a debt-broker of knives who buys unfulfilled contracts from the dead and calls them in. Jorren's name is on the paper as the target.","ln":["Jorren: They made me a knife before I was a person."]},"mirrorself":{"n":"The Other Wound","bg":"The Other Wound is the Mirror Road's guardian, made of every self a traveller ran from. For Kaelren it is the boy who never stops charging; for Jorren it is the knife that never became a person. The Mirror Road is old Shadowdeep-and-Stormreach border country, used to test couriers before they were trusted with secrets. It cannot be beaten alone, which is the point.","ln":["Border saying: Walk the Mirror Road with a friend, or do not come back."]},"the other wound":{"n":"The Other Wound","bg":"The Other Wound is the Mirror Road's guardian, made of every self a traveller ran from. For Kaelren it is the boy who never stops charging; for Jorren it is the knife that never became a person. The Mirror Road is old Shadowdeep-and-Stormreach border country, used to test couriers before they were trusted with secrets. It cannot be beaten alone, which is the point.","ln":["Border saying: Walk the Mirror Road with a friend, or do not come back."]},"hush1":{"n":"The First Silence","bg":"The First Silence is the Void's first quiet in the Greywater, a village of lit windows where nobody remembers having been anything else. It speaks one word, 'Stop', because the Void wants everything to stop moving, since everything hurts when it moves. The Voyager walks with you and will not use magic. Whatever it is, it is afraid, and that does not make it safe.","ln":["The Voyager: I do not hate it. That is the difficult part."]},"the first silence":{"n":"The First Silence","bg":"The First Silence is the Void's first quiet in the Greywater, a village of lit windows where nobody remembers having been anything else. It speaks one word, 'Stop', because the Void wants everything to stop moving, since everything hurts when it moves. The Voyager walks with you and will not use magic. Whatever it is, it is afraid, and that does not make it safe.","ln":["The Voyager: I do not hate it. That is the difficult part."]},"the voyager's shadow":{"n":"The Voyager's Shadow","bg":"The Voyager's Shadow waits on the grey road to the place where the Codex stops writing. It is not the Voyager, and nobody can say what made it. The Voyager is the one Councillor without a Realm, the Hammer-Bearer, uncanny and kind, and his true name is unknown. The Shadow tries to tell you who he is, and lies.","ln":["The Voyager: Everyone has a line in it, and I do not."]},"voidmirror":{"n":"The Voyager's Shadow","bg":"The Voyager's Shadow waits on the grey road to the place where the Codex stops writing. It is not the Voyager, and nobody can say what made it. The Voyager is the one Councillor without a Realm, the Hammer-Bearer, uncanny and kind, and his true name is unknown. The Shadow tries to tell you who he is, and lies.","ln":["The Voyager: Everyone has a line in it, and I do not."]},"the unmade":{"n":"The Unmade","bg":"The Unmade is the stretch of road the Void took whole: not a village, a road, so that no one ever walked it. It is the Void stirring rather than conquering, one of the first fractures drawn north by the coming war. What fights you is the absence itself, holding until the Realms decide to march. It does not want the road; it wants nothing to move along it.","ln":["Stormreach rider: Speed is what the Void cannot match. Prove it."]},"unmade":{"n":"The Unmade","bg":"The Unmade is the stretch of road the Void took whole: not a village, a road, so that no one ever walked it. It is the Void stirring rather than conquering, one of the first fractures drawn north by the coming war. What fights you is the absence itself, holding until the Realms decide to march. It does not want the road; it wants nothing to move along it.","ln":["Stormreach rider: Speed is what the Void cannot match. Prove it."]},"hollowking":{"n":"The Herald","bg":"The Herald on the Hollow Throne wears the faces of everyone the Void has taken a name from. The Void wants a seat close enough to watch the boy; it does not want the world. Every Realm that still answers sends someone. Push it back and whatever happens at the Iron Throne happens without the Void leaning on it.","ln":["Lord Hald: This is the last road before the north."]},"the shard-keeper":{"n":"The Shard-Keeper","bg":"The Shard-Keeper guards the first starmetal splinter in a vault the Assize sealed and filed under a word nobody uses. The four shards fell from the sky when the ninth Realm collapsed at its birth, and they hold a fragment of raw possibility, which is why they feel heavier than they are. The Keeper is the vault's own law, made of the oath sworn when it was sealed. It yields only to the page that names it.","ln":["Assize index: SEE UNDER 'ELSEWHERE'."]},"shardkeeper":{"n":"The Shard-Keeper","bg":"The Shard-Keeper guards the first starmetal splinter in a vault the Assize sealed and filed under a word nobody uses. The four shards fell from the sky when the ninth Realm collapsed at its birth, and they hold a fragment of raw possibility, which is why they feel heavier than they are. The Keeper is the vault's own law, made of the oath sworn when it was sealed. It yields only to the page that names it.","ln":["Assize index: SEE UNDER 'ELSEWHERE'."]},"galewyrm":{"n":"The Gale-Warden","bg":"The Gale-Warden is the wind that keeps the second starmetal shard in a Stormreach tower no one has climbed. The Free Assembly argued for most of a millennium about who owned it and never agreed, so the wind kept it for them. The shard sings when air passes through it. The Warden is not a guard; it is the argument, still blowing.","ln":["Orrin: Stormreach does not keep things. We lose them to the wind."]},"the gale-warden":{"n":"The Gale-Warden","bg":"The Gale-Warden is the wind that keeps the second starmetal shard in a Stormreach tower no one has climbed. The Free Assembly argued for most of a millennium about who owned it and never agreed, so the wind kept it for them. The shard sings when air passes through it. The Warden is not a guard; it is the argument, still blowing.","ln":["Orrin: Stormreach does not keep things. We lose them to the wind."]},"the keep's memory":{"n":"The Keep's Memory","bg":"The Keep's Memory is the third starmetal shard's guardian, the warmth in the stone beneath Gravenhall that the Hold-Senate mistook for its own heartbeat for decades. It is made of every Stonehold soldier who leaned on the shard's warmth during a siege and did not know why the wall held. Taking the shard may bring the Keep down. Leaving it invites the Dominion.","ln":["Senator Orm: I felt it in the floor and thought it was my heart."]},"deepshard":{"n":"The Keep's Memory","bg":"The Keep's Memory is the third starmetal shard's guardian, the warmth in the stone beneath Gravenhall that the Hold-Senate mistook for its own heartbeat for decades. It is made of every Stonehold soldier who leaned on the shard's warmth during a siege and did not know why the wall held. Taking the shard may bring the Keep down. Leaving it invites the Dominion.","ln":["Senator Orm: I felt it in the floor and thought it was my heart."]},"the first smith":{"n":"The First Smith","bg":"The First Smith stands at the Forge Before Forges, striking at nothing, trying to make a sound he heard once. He was a mortal smith, the first to work the starmetal that fell when the ninth Realm collapsed, and he paid for every blow with warmth until he had none. He is not a creator and made no worlds; he only heard the Shard break. The Shardmaul is not the Hammer; it only remembers it.","ln":["Forge inscription: Strike once for the broken Realm. Strike again for the cost."]},"hammerghost":{"n":"The First Smith","bg":"The First Smith stands at the Forge Before Forges, striking at nothing, trying to make a sound he heard once. He was a mortal smith, the first to work the starmetal that fell when the ninth Realm collapsed, and he paid for every blow with warmth until he had none. He is not a creator and made no worlds; he only heard the Shard break. The Shardmaul is not the Hammer; it only remembers it.","ln":["Forge inscription: Strike once for the broken Realm. Strike again for the cost."]},"quarryking":{"n":"The Petrified Foreman","bg":"The Petrified Foreman cast too much Gravem in a week to hold the pit wall for his crew, and turned to stone in days instead of decades. His hold keeps his whole crew stone with him, waiting. He is stone to the neck and weeping. He asks that his wife never learn how long he held.","ln":["Mason Carrow: Most take twenty years. He took a week."]},"the petrified foreman":{"n":"The Petrified Foreman","bg":"The Petrified Foreman cast too much Gravem in a week to hold the pit wall for his crew, and turned to stone in days instead of decades. His hold keeps his whole crew stone with him, waiting. He is stone to the neck and weeping. He asks that his wife never learn how long he held.","ln":["Mason Carrow: Most take twenty years. He took a week."]},"senator of stone":{"n":"Senator of Stone","bg":"Senator of Stone is Senator Orsk, who turned to stone two years ago and whose seat still votes. In Stonehold, Senators serve for life and the chamber obeys precedent, and nothing in precedent covers a stone senator. His votes are slow and always no. The Senate cannot remove him without breaking the oldest rule it has.","ln":["Senatrix Ildra: I have voted four thousand times. I remember eleven of them."]},"senatorstone":{"n":"Senator of Stone","bg":"Senator of Stone is Senator Orsk, who turned to stone two years ago and whose seat still votes. In Stonehold, Senators serve for life and the chamber obeys precedent, and nothing in precedent covers a stone senator. His votes are slow and always no. The Senate cannot remove him without breaking the oldest rule it has.","ln":["Senatrix Ildra: I have voted four thousand times. I remember eleven of them."]},"the stone king":{"n":"The Stone King","bg":"The Stone King is the guardian at the top of the Vow Stair, ten thousand steps each carved with the name of a soldier who held a line. He is the first soldier who swore the Granite Vow, held until the stone held him, and became the stair's last step. He tests outsiders who climb by asking them every name. He does not care about blood, only duty.","ln":["The Granite Vow: Hold until the stone holds you."]},"stoneking":{"n":"The Stone King","bg":"The Stone King is the guardian at the top of the Vow Stair, ten thousand steps each carved with the name of a soldier who held a line. He is the first soldier who swore the Granite Vow, held until the stone held him, and became the stair's last step. He tests outsiders who climb by asking them every name. He does not care about blood, only duty.","ln":["The Granite Vow: Hold until the stone holds you."]},"the still seer":{"n":"The Still Seer","bg":"The Still Seer was a Frostgrave prophet who saw so far that he stopped moving mid-sentence, and Chrona's numbness took the rest of him. The Prophet Court keeps him in the Still Observatory because everything he says comes true and nobody dares to finish listening. His last sentence ends with a name. He has been waiting for its owner.","ln":["Prophet Iseult: It is so tiring to be right."]},"stillseer":{"n":"The Still Seer","bg":"The Still Seer was a Frostgrave prophet who saw so far that he stopped moving mid-sentence, and Chrona's numbness took the rest of him. The Prophet Court keeps him in the Still Observatory because everything he says comes true and nobody dares to finish listening. His last sentence ends with a name. He has been waiting for its owner.","ln":["Prophet Iseult: It is so tiring to be right."]},"frozenfuture":{"n":"The Frozen Future","bg":"The Frozen Future is the Winter Aisle given a will, the place where the Prophet Court freezes possible futures so it can read them. It has grown for a century because every seer who reads a door feels a little less and freezes another. It is not cruel. It is a library that has forgotten the difference between keeping and closing.","ln":["Frostgrave saying: A future read is a future spent."]},"the frozen future":{"n":"The Frozen Future","bg":"The Frozen Future is the Winter Aisle given a will, the place where the Prophet Court freezes possible futures so it can read them. It has grown for a century because every seer who reads a door feels a little less and freezes another. It is not cruel. It is a library that has forgotten the difference between keeping and closing.","ln":["Frostgrave saying: A future read is a future spent."]},"facelessone":{"n":"The Faceless One","bg":"The Faceless One works the unmaking in the Quiet Warehouse, where Umbral Councillors are taken out of the world so that their chairs are empty and nobody remembers who sat there. It is an agent, not the Unmaker: a Shadowdeep spark-ripper hired to do the work and paid in names. It has no face because it gave its own as a deposit. Vesper's envoy credentials do not impress it.","ln":["Note in Vesper's hand: Do not trust the one you trust most."]},"the faceless one":{"n":"The Faceless One","bg":"The Faceless One works the unmaking in the Quiet Warehouse, where Umbral Councillors are taken out of the world so that their chairs are empty and nobody remembers who sat there. It is an agent, not the Unmaker: a Shadowdeep spark-ripper hired to do the work and paid in names. It has no face because it gave its own as a deposit. Vesper's envoy credentials do not impress it.","ln":["Note in Vesper's hand: Do not trust the one you trust most."]},"unmaker":{"n":"The Unmaker","bg":"The Unmaker is a Council elder who feared being forgotten more than being hated, and unmade the others so that someone would at least remember him. Shadowdeep's price is identity erosion, and he paid it in full, taking the identities of others to refill his own. He hides in Hush's hidden depths behind transparent walls. He is pitiful and very dangerous.","ln":["Vesper: He unmade the others so that someone would remember him."]},"the unmaker":{"n":"The Unmaker","bg":"The Unmaker is a Council elder who feared being forgotten more than being hated, and unmade the others so that someone would at least remember him. Shadowdeep's price is identity erosion, and he paid it in full, taking the identities of others to refill his own. He hides in Hush's hidden depths behind transparent walls. He is pitiful and very dangerous.","ln":["Vesper: He unmade the others so that someone would remember him."]},"the brass auditor":{"n":"The Brass Auditor","bg":"The Brass Auditor is the Counting House's guardian engine, built to keep the Dominion's ledger of every worker traded as a unit of output. It is Auditor Brass's former partner, a man who gave the synthetic spark everything but his arithmetic. The ledger records a hundred thousand names, each with a number. The Auditor protects the numbers.","ln":["Auditor Brass: I count lives in units of productivity. Please do not ask me what a life is worth."]},"brassauditor":{"n":"The Brass Auditor","bg":"The Brass Auditor is the Counting House's guardian engine, built to keep the Dominion's ledger of every worker traded as a unit of output. It is Auditor Brass's former partner, a man who gave the synthetic spark everything but his arithmetic. The ledger records a hundred thousand names, each with a number. The Auditor protects the numbers.","ln":["Auditor Brass: I count lives in units of productivity. Please do not ask me what a life is worth."]},"unit seven":{"n":"Unit Seven","bg":"Unit Seven was a man, once, a Spark Yard foreman whose synthetic spark took his humanity a little at a time and finally his name. He speaks in numbers now. Auditor Brass knew him. At the end he says a number that may be his birthday.","ln":["Brass: Bury him under his own name, not his number."]},"unitseven":{"n":"Unit Seven","bg":"Unit Seven was a man, once, a Spark Yard foreman whose synthetic spark took his humanity a little at a time and finally his name. He speaks in numbers now. Auditor Brass knew him. At the end he says a number that may be his birthday.","ln":["Brass: Bury him under his own name, not his number."]},"the unrecorded":{"n":"The Unrecorded","bg":"The Unrecorded is the thing that wears the gap where Captain Sera used to be in the Lanthys Vale records. When the Council struck her out, the city agreed so quickly that the agreement itself took a shape. It is made of everyone who looked away. Elyndra remembers her because a Lantern archivist keeps what others are relieved to drop.","ln":["Chalk on the plaza, later: SERA. ASK."]},"unrecorded":{"n":"The Unrecorded","bg":"The Unrecorded is the thing that wears the gap where Captain Sera used to be in the Lanthys Vale records. When the Council struck her out, the city agreed so quickly that the agreement itself took a shape. It is made of everyone who looked away. Elyndra remembers her because a Lantern archivist keeps what others are relieved to drop.","ln":["Chalk on the plaza, later: SERA. ASK."]},"the agreeable":{"n":"The Agreeable","bg":"The Agreeable is the Market That Agreed: a street where a merchant stopped mid-sentence, looked at his son, said 'from storage' and everyone breathed out and went on. It is the Void's gentlest work, a market that makes room for the wrong thing rather than fight it. Kaelren cannot charge at it. What breaks it is someone asking a stranger's name, and listening.","ln":["Market stall chalk: ASK THEIR NAMES."]},"agreeable":{"n":"The Agreeable","bg":"The Agreeable is the Market That Agreed: a street where a merchant stopped mid-sentence, looked at his son, said 'from storage' and everyone breathed out and went on. It is the Void's gentlest work, a market that makes room for the wrong thing rather than fight it. Kaelren cannot charge at it. What breaks it is someone asking a stranger's name, and listening.","ln":["Market stall chalk: ASK THEIR NAMES."]},"the unconfirmed":{"n":"The Unconfirmed","bg":"The Unconfirmed is the corridor in the Keep that people walk around without knowing why, which the Codex has no page for. It is a fold made by something the Codex could not classify, and it has started to defend itself. The Voyager walks it with you because it has no page for him either. If anything in there tries to tell you his name, it is lying.","ln":["The Voyager: The Codex has no page for it. It has none for me either, so I thought we might get on."]},"unconfirmed":{"n":"The Unconfirmed","bg":"The Unconfirmed is the corridor in the Keep that people walk around without knowing why, which the Codex has no page for. It is a fold made by something the Codex could not classify, and it has started to defend itself. The Voyager walks it with you because it has no page for him either. If anything in there tries to tell you his name, it is lying.","ln":["The Voyager: The Codex has no page for it. It has none for me either, so I thought we might get on."]},"hunter prime":{"n":"Hunter Prime","bg":"Hunter Prime leads the Hunters in the Codex Vaults, constructs the Codex made to hunt whatever it cannot write. They are waking because the Codex has met something it has not classified. Hunter Prime is the oldest, the one that writes small things on everything it passes. It hesitates when it looks at you.","ln":["Councillor, quoted by Hald: The system is responding to something it has not classified."]},"hunterprime":{"n":"Hunter Prime","bg":"Hunter Prime leads the Hunters in the Codex Vaults, constructs the Codex made to hunt whatever it cannot write. They are waking because the Codex has met something it has not classified. Hunter Prime is the oldest, the one that writes small things on everything it passes. It hesitates when it looks at you.","ln":["Councillor, quoted by Hald: The system is responding to something it has not classified."]},"kingsignal":{"n":"A Signal Wearing a Crown","bg":"A Signal Wearing a Crown is not a creature. It is a label that appeared for one breath in the deep Codex, CROWNED PRESENCE: UNCONFIRMED. FUTURE LAYER, and the stretch of road where the noise of a skirmish was removed. Lyriel calls it a future, far off in years rather than miles. What you fight is the silence it leaves; what it says, it says gently, and offers rest.","ln":["Codex margin, struck out: — UNCONFIRMED —"]},"a signal wearing a crown":{"n":"A Signal Wearing a Crown","bg":"A Signal Wearing a Crown is not a creature. It is a label that appeared for one breath in the deep Codex, CROWNED PRESENCE: UNCONFIRMED. FUTURE LAYER, and the stretch of road where the noise of a skirmish was removed. Lyriel calls it a future, far off in years rather than miles. What you fight is the silence it leaves; what it says, it says gently, and offers rest.","ln":["Codex margin, struck out: — UNCONFIRMED —"]},"gatewatcher":{"n":"The Watcher at the Gate","bg":"The Watcher at the Gate stands at a grey edge that opened above the old square the size of a door. It is the Void looking back. Grey shapes drift between the houses and watch the children with no more hostility than weather. It does not strike, and nobody is afraid while it looks, which is the worst part.","ln":["Aeris: Nobody has taught me how to fight something that does not fight."]},"the watcher at the gate":{"n":"The Watcher at the Gate","bg":"The Watcher at the Gate stands at a grey edge that opened above the old square the size of a door. It is the Void looking back. Grey shapes drift between the houses and watch the children with no more hostility than weather. It does not strike, and nobody is afraid while it looks, which is the worst part.","ln":["Aeris: Nobody has taught me how to fight something that does not fight."]},"the crowned presence":{"n":"The Crowned Presence","bg":"The Crowned Presence is a prophetic vision: a grey edge over a crossroads market opening onto a hall of ash and a crowned figure, far off in years. Lyriel says it is a future, not a guest. It is unnamed and must stay unnamed. It hesitates when it sees you, as if it has met someone earlier than expected.","ln":["Bread-seller: We finished packing first. Then we looked."]},"harrowedking":{"n":"The Crowned Presence","bg":"The Crowned Presence is a prophetic vision: a grey edge over a crossroads market opening onto a hall of ash and a crowned figure, far off in years. Lyriel says it is a future, not a guest. It is unnamed and must stay unnamed. It hesitates when it sees you, as if it has met someone earlier than expected.","ln":["Bread-seller: We finished packing first. Then we looked."]},"sh_listener":{"n":"The Listener","bg":"The Listener guards the Listening Room, a cellar whose floor remembers every step and whose walls have ears of black glass. It is the Order's first test. It was an initiate who failed the crossing and stayed to listen for the next one. The silence is a bargain; it takes your name first.","ln":["A quiet courier: If you have to fight, you have already failed. You may still survive."]},"the listener":{"n":"The Listener","bg":"The Listener guards the Listening Room, a cellar whose floor remembers every step and whose walls have ears of black glass. It is the Order's first test. It was an initiate who failed the crossing and stayed to listen for the next one. The silence is a bargain; it takes your name first.","ln":["A quiet courier: If you have to fight, you have already failed. You may still survive."]},"sh_floor":{"n":"Floor-Mistress Thessaly","bg":"Floor-Mistress Thessaly teaches the Nightingale Hall's floors to stay silent. She has trained Nightblades for thirty years and given up most of her own name to the Order. The first secret she teaches is that the loudest thing in a room is the intent. She spars hard because she likes you.","ln":["Thessaly: Not wanting is the hardest thing in the world."]},"floor-mistress thessaly":{"n":"Floor-Mistress Thessaly","bg":"Floor-Mistress Thessaly teaches the Nightingale Hall's floors to stay silent. She has trained Nightblades for thirty years and given up most of her own name to the Order. The first secret she teaches is that the loudest thing in a room is the intent. She spars hard because she likes you.","ln":["Thessaly: Not wanting is the hardest thing in the world."]},"sh_toll":{"n":"Tollmaster Orsk","bg":"Tollmaster Orsk taxes the roads past Hush and sends none of it home. He is a Shadowdeep official who decided the Umbral Council would never notice a few tolls. The Order noticed. He is the first contract; how you finish it is the first choice the Order watches.","ln":["The courier: The second coin is for the sleep. You will want it."]},"tollmaster orsk":{"n":"Tollmaster Orsk","bg":"Tollmaster Orsk taxes the roads past Hush and sends none of it home. He is a Shadowdeep official who decided the Umbral Council would never notice a few tolls. The Order noticed. He is the first contract; how you finish it is the first choice the Order watches.","ln":["The courier: The second coin is for the sleep. You will want it."]},"sh_fang":{"n":"Fang Initiate Brask","bg":"Fang Initiate Brask is a Fang-school initiate who refused to leave the Pit. The Fang kills in the open; the Veil kills in silence; they are rival schools of the same Order. He is a boy who wants to be dangerous and is not yet. Rook wants him beaten in front of everyone.","ln":["Fang-Sergeant Rook: The Fang does not forget, though it will forgive if you pay."]},"fang initiate brask":{"n":"Fang Initiate Brask","bg":"Fang Initiate Brask is a Fang-school initiate who refused to leave the Pit. The Fang kills in the open; the Veil kills in silence; they are rival schools of the same Order. He is a boy who wants to be dangerous and is not yet. Rook wants him beaten in front of everyone.","ln":["Fang-Sergeant Rook: The Fang does not forget, though it will forgive if you pay."]},"sh_namewarden":{"n":"The Name-Warden","bg":"The Name-Warden stands at the door of the Hall of Erased Names and takes the keeping of your name. Every Nightblade is, in the end, a hole in a shape. He is the oldest Nightblade alive and has given away so many names he is mostly door.","ln":["Veilrend's Voice: You will be given another name that no one else may use."]},"the name-warden":{"n":"The Name-Warden","bg":"The Name-Warden stands at the door of the Hall of Erased Names and takes the keeping of your name. Every Nightblade is, in the end, a hole in a shape. He is the oldest Nightblade alive and has given away so many names he is mostly door.","ln":["Veilrend's Voice: You will be given another name that no one else may use."]},"the deep walker":{"n":"The Deep Walker","bg":"The Deep Walker was a courier who has walked the Black Underway since before the first Realm Bridge. The Underways connect Lightforge's cellars to Flameborne's forges to Frostgrave's ice. He lets you pass if you can make him. Not every man comes up the other side whole; that cost is the road's.","ln":["Underway-Guide Pale: The roads are for people who want to be seen."]},"sh_deepwalker":{"n":"The Deep Walker","bg":"The Deep Walker was a courier who has walked the Black Underway since before the first Realm Bridge. The Underways connect Lightforge's cellars to Flameborne's forges to Frostgrave's ice. He lets you pass if you can make him. Not every man comes up the other side whole; that cost is the road's.","ln":["Underway-Guide Pale: The roads are for people who want to be seen."]},"the one who unmakes":{"n":"The One Who Unmakes","bg":"The One Who Unmakes wears a Councillor's robe and has been unmaking Umbral Councillors because the Council had begun hunting its own to keep its secrets. It was trying to make the Council less of a thing worth hunting. It was not wrong. Identity erosion is Shadowdeep's price; this one spent other people's.","ln":["An Umbral Councillor: Knowledge in this city is a kind of debt."]},"sh_unmaker":{"n":"The One Who Unmakes","bg":"The One Who Unmakes wears a Councillor's robe and has been unmaking Umbral Councillors because the Council had begun hunting its own to keep its secrets. It was trying to make the Council less of a thing worth hunting. It was not wrong. Identity erosion is Shadowdeep's price; this one spent other people's.","ln":["An Umbral Councillor: Knowledge in this city is a kind of debt."]},"sh_seven":{"n":"Councillor Seven","bg":"Councillor Seven is the last of the old guard, buying the Order's loyalty one name at a time so he can rule alone. Six chairs are empty; his hums. He has unmade so much of himself that only ambition is left.","ln":["Hush saying: In Shadowdeep, nothing is ever said aloud."]},"councillor seven":{"n":"Councillor Seven","bg":"Councillor Seven is the last of the old guard, buying the Order's loyalty one name at a time so he can rule alone. Six chairs are empty; his hums. He has unmade so much of himself that only ambition is left.","ln":["Hush saying: In Shadowdeep, nothing is ever said aloud."]},"sh_veilrend":{"n":"Veilrend, Hollowed","bg":"Veilrend, Hollowed, is the head of the Order after fifty years of unmaking himself for it. There is nothing left in the chair but the habit of command. His last contract is to be ended, quietly, because he has been quiet all his life.","ln":["Veilrend's Voice: Mercy and cruelty are one blade."]},"veilrend, hollowed":{"n":"Veilrend, Hollowed","bg":"Veilrend, Hollowed, is the head of the Order after fifty years of unmaking himself for it. There is nothing left in the chair but the habit of command. His last contract is to be ended, quietly, because he has been quiet all his life.","ln":["Veilrend's Voice: Mercy and cruelty are one blade."]},"sh_vaultwarden":{"n":"The Vault Warden","bg":"The Vault Warden guards seven smiling masks in the Council's vault; the Vault gives one to anyone who walks in and is not turned away. It is the Umbral Council's oldest secret, the reason its rulers have never had names. Take a mask, or break them and let Shadowdeep be seen.","ln":["Vault inscription: A crown with no face fits anyone."]},"the vault warden":{"n":"The Vault Warden","bg":"The Vault Warden guards seven smiling masks in the Council's vault; the Vault gives one to anyone who walks in and is not turned away. It is the Umbral Council's oldest secret, the reason its rulers have never had names. Take a mask, or break them and let Shadowdeep be seen.","ln":["Vault inscription: A crown with no face fits anyone."]},"the well-keeper":{"n":"The Well-Keeper","bg":"The Well-Keeper was never hired. It is what a village makes without meaning to when fear sits in one place too long: the iron taste came first, then the rumour, then the thing at the bottom that fed on both. It wears the face of the last person who drowned there, because that is the face Ashford was most afraid of. When the fear goes, it goes with it.","ln":["Pim: The horses knew first. Horses always know first."]},"sb_ash_well":{"n":"The Well-Keeper","bg":"The Well-Keeper was never hired. It is what a village makes without meaning to when fear sits in one place too long: the iron taste came first, then the rumour, then the thing at the bottom that fed on both. It wears the face of the last person who drowned there, because that is the face Ashford was most afraid of. When the fear goes, it goes with it.","ln":["Pim: The horses knew first. Horses always know first."]},"sb_ash_wolves":{"n":"The Grey Alpha","bg":"The Grey Alpha's pack hunts in silence because something taught it that noise is waste. The grey-thread collar around its neck matches the grey wax on Dominion seals, and nobody in Ashford can say who fastened it. One reading: a Metalcrown handler trained the pack for quiet raids and lost it; the silence is training, not the Void. That keeps the Void stirring rather than served, as canon wants.","ln":["Tamsin: A collar is a promise somebody made to a dog. I want to meet who made this one."]},"the grey alpha":{"n":"The Grey Alpha","bg":"The Grey Alpha's pack hunts in silence because something taught it that noise is waste. The grey-thread collar around its neck matches the grey wax on Dominion seals, and nobody in Ashford can say who fastened it. One reading: a Metalcrown handler trained the pack for quiet raids and lost it; the silence is training, not the Void. That keeps the Void stirring rather than served, as canon wants.","ln":["Tamsin: A collar is a promise somebody made to a dog. I want to meet who made this one."]},"the barker":{"n":"The Barker","bg":"The Barker runs a fair that only exists after dark on the old common, a stall-row of lanterns left lit by people who could not let go of someone who walked away. Marrin's husband did walk to it nineteen years ago, and the fair kept his place out of habit. The Barker calls the crowd because calling is all it is. It holds no one; it only keeps the seats warm.","ln":["Children's chant: Roll up, roll up, the fair for the gone, the lanterns are lit and the music plays on."]},"sb_ash_bride":{"n":"The Barker","bg":"The Barker runs a fair that only exists after dark on the old common, a stall-row of lanterns left lit by people who could not let go of someone who walked away. Marrin's husband did walk to it nineteen years ago, and the fair kept his place out of habit. The Barker calls the crowd because calling is all it is. It holds no one; it only keeps the seats warm.","ln":["Children's chant: Roll up, roll up, the fair for the gone, the lanterns are lit and the music plays on."]},"sb_ash_scribe":{"n":"The Dead Clerk","bg":"The Dead Clerk was Ashford's postmaster when the Dominion closed the roads, and he swore he would not let a single letter leave without a proper stamp. The stamps never came. He died at the counter and kept his oath, guarding a hundred letters from hands that were not official. He is not hostile, only exact.","ln":["Stamp on every letter: HELD PENDING ROAD.","Orlo: He initialled his own death notice. I checked."]},"the dead clerk":{"n":"The Dead Clerk","bg":"The Dead Clerk was Ashford's postmaster when the Dominion closed the roads, and he swore he would not let a single letter leave without a proper stamp. The stamps never came. He died at the counter and kept his oath, guarding a hundred letters from hands that were not official. He is not hostile, only exact.","ln":["Stamp on every letter: HELD PENDING ROAD.","Orlo: He initialled his own death notice. I checked."]},"sb_emb_anvil":{"n":"The Slag-Born","bg":"The Slag-Born is the ghost of Brogan's master smith, who died at the anvil with a blade half-drawn. In Hearthmount, smiths say a forge remembers every blade it made, and this one would not let him stop until the last was finished. He rings the anvil at night because his hands still think they are working. Finishing the blade is the only rest he understands.","ln":["Foundry custom: Never let the last hammer-stroke be someone else's."]},"the slag-born":{"n":"The Slag-Born","bg":"The Slag-Born is the ghost of Brogan's master smith, who died at the anvil with a blade half-drawn. In Hearthmount, smiths say a forge remembers every blade it made, and this one would not let him stop until the last was finished. He rings the anvil at night because his hands still think they are working. Finishing the blade is the only rest he understands.","ln":["Foundry custom: Never let the last hammer-stroke be someone else's."]},"sb_emb_duel":{"n":"The Unbroken Duellist","bg":"The Unbroken Duellist was an ordinary Hearthmount brawler who found that Embercraft grew in him every time a crowd shouted. Forty challengers later he cannot lose, and he cannot stop: the fire feeds on the fight and takes another piece of his restraint with each win. Underneath he is a tired man who would like to sit down. Beating him gently is the only mercy the ring allows.","ln":["Ring call: Shout for him and he burns. Go quiet and he lives."]},"the unbroken duellist":{"n":"The Unbroken Duellist","bg":"The Unbroken Duellist was an ordinary Hearthmount brawler who found that Embercraft grew in him every time a crowd shouted. Forty challengers later he cannot lose, and he cannot stop: the fire feeds on the fight and takes another piece of his restraint with each win. Underneath he is a tired man who would like to sit down. Beating him gently is the only mercy the ring allows.","ln":["Ring call: Shout for him and he burns. Go quiet and he lives."]},"the vault-thief":{"n":"The Vault-Thief","bg":"The Vault-Thief is the old king's sister, the Flameborne King's aunt, who was passed over and then forgotten in a Court that only honours what it sees. She has slipped the crown out of the vault once a year for a century of Court rumour, and this year someone noticed. She wanted to be seen wearing it, once, by anyone. The crown is the smallest part of what she took back.","ln":["Liss: She put it back every year. We just never looked."]},"sb_emb_crown":{"n":"The Vault-Thief","bg":"The Vault-Thief is the old king's sister, the Flameborne King's aunt, who was passed over and then forgotten in a Court that only honours what it sees. She has slipped the crown out of the vault once a year for a century of Court rumour, and this year someone noticed. She wanted to be seen wearing it, once, by anyone. The crown is the smallest part of what she took back.","ln":["Liss: She put it back every year. We just never looked."]},"the road-warden":{"n":"The Road-Warden","bg":"The Road-Warden was a pilgrim who walked to the Ember Throne so many times that the road became the only place he knew how to be. He stopped others passing because, in his mind, arriving means leaving the road. The Flameborne King once walked the Cinder Road with him and shared bread at the halfway cairn. He remembers the bread, not the King.","ln":["Pilgrim's cairn-mark: Further every year. Walk anyway."]},"sb_emb_cinder":{"n":"The Road-Warden","bg":"The Road-Warden was a pilgrim who walked to the Ember Throne so many times that the road became the only place he knew how to be. He stopped others passing because, in his mind, arriving means leaving the road. The Flameborne King once walked the Cinder Road with him and shared bread at the halfway cairn. He remembers the bread, not the King.","ln":["Pilgrim's cairn-mark: Further every year. Walk anyway."]},"sb_emb_ember2":{"n":"The Unlit Engine","bg":"The Unlit Engine is a spark-reactor Metalcrown traders left in a Hearthmount annex ten years ago, part of a trial nobody admits to now. Something in it kept the pattern of a child, a copy taken from whoever was used to tune it. It warms itself on memory because there is no fuel left. Hearthmount smiths who fed it once now cross the street to avoid the annex door.","ln":["Jun: It hums when I read to it. Same three notes. Like it is asking for the next page."]},"the unlit engine":{"n":"The Unlit Engine","bg":"The Unlit Engine is a spark-reactor Metalcrown traders left in a Hearthmount annex ten years ago, part of a trial nobody admits to now. Something in it kept the pattern of a child, a copy taken from whoever was used to tune it. It warms itself on memory because there is no fuel left. Hearthmount smiths who fed it once now cross the street to avoid the annex door.","ln":["Jun: It hums when I read to it. Same three notes. Like it is asking for the next page."]},"the face-taker":{"n":"The Face-Taker","bg":"The Face-Taker takes faces from Hush's sleepers and hangs masks over the gaps in the Gallery of Masks. It is a failed Nightblade who gave up his own face to the Order and has been trying on others since. It does not want them; it wants one that fits.","ln":["Hush saying: I would give you my name. I do not have one today."]},"sb_unm_face":{"n":"The Face-Taker","bg":"The Face-Taker takes faces from Hush's sleepers and hangs masks over the gaps in the Gallery of Masks. It is a failed Nightblade who gave up his own face to the Order and has been trying on others since. It does not want them; it wants one that fits.","ln":["Hush saying: I would give you my name. I do not have one today."]},"the gatekeeper of nothing":{"n":"The Gatekeeper of Nothing","bg":"The Gatekeeper of Nothing guards a door with no house, which opens on the room where Hush stores everything it wants to forget. It breathes. It is the city's forgetting given a hinge.","ln":["Ney: Every door in this city opens somewhere. Most close behind you."]},"sb_unm_door":{"n":"The Gatekeeper of Nothing","bg":"The Gatekeeper of Nothing guards a door with no house, which opens on the room where Hush stores everything it wants to forget. It breathes. It is the city's forgetting given a hinge.","ln":["Ney: Every door in this city opens somewhere. Most close behind you."]},"sb_unm_whisper":{"n":"The Rumour-Eater","bg":"The Rumour-Eater is fed rumours so the city will look away from the real unmaking. It eats them and grows, and returns them in other mouths. Someone in the Council fed it on purpose.","ln":["Vae: A rumour is just a letter nobody signed."]},"the rumour-eater":{"n":"The Rumour-Eater","bg":"The Rumour-Eater is fed rumours so the city will look away from the real unmaking. It eats them and grows, and returns them in other mouths. Someone in the Council fed it on purpose.","ln":["Vae: A rumour is just a letter nobody signed."]},"the stayed reflection":{"n":"The Stayed Reflection","bg":"The Stayed Reflection is someone's reflection that would not leave the Hall of Echoes after eleven years and has begun to act on its own. Identity erosion works both ways; the owner lost some, and the reflection found it. It has started combing its hair a different way, and it fights anyone who tries to pull it back into the glass.","ln":["Sol: A cracked mirror still shows you. Just not all of you."]},"sb_unm_mirror":{"n":"The Stayed Reflection","bg":"The Stayed Reflection is someone's reflection that would not leave the Hall of Echoes after eleven years and has begun to act on its own. Identity erosion works both ways; the owner lost some, and the reflection found it. It has started combing its hair a different way, and it fights anyone who tries to pull it back into the glass.","ln":["Sol: A cracked mirror still shows you. Just not all of you."]},"sb_unm_veil":{"n":"The Veil-Tearer","bg":"The Veil-Tearer walks through a rip in the air above the Torn Roads: something that used to be the city's memory and does not know it is dead. Not Void, not yet. It asks for a name.","ln":["Ruth: The Veil does not protect us. It remembers us."]},"the veil-tearer":{"n":"The Veil-Tearer","bg":"The Veil-Tearer walks through a rip in the air above the Torn Roads: something that used to be the city's memory and does not know it is dead. Not Void, not yet. It asks for a name.","ln":["Ruth: The Veil does not protect us. It remembers us."]},"the forgotten flame":{"n":"The Forgotten Flame","bg":"The Forgotten Flame is one of the great Citadel lamps lit at Meridian's founding to witness the city's oaths. Radiance does not forget, and the lamp has begun naming every person it ever lit for, four hundred of them, many long dead. The Council wants it silenced because some of those names were struck from the record for convenience. The flame is not angry, only accurate.","ln":["Ida: I wrote down every name. It took the whole night."]},"sb_lum_lamp":{"n":"The Forgotten Flame","bg":"The Forgotten Flame is one of the great Citadel lamps lit at Meridian's founding to witness the city's oaths. Radiance does not forget, and the lamp has begun naming every person it ever lit for, four hundred of them, many long dead. The Council wants it silenced because some of those names were struck from the record for convenience. The flame is not angry, only accurate.","ln":["Ida: I wrote down every name. It took the whole night."]},"the glass-master":{"n":"The Glass-Master","bg":"The Glass-Master runs an unlicensed foundry in Meridian that buys memory-glass and recuts it for the market, and lately has been drawing fresh glass from the dying without asking. Spiritveil draws memory-glass with consent and ceremony; the Glass-Master skips both. He believes testimony is a public good and the dying owe it. Forty families disagree.","ln":["Market saying: Glass from the Vale is clear. Glass from the cellar is cheap."]},"sb_lum_memory":{"n":"The Glass-Master","bg":"The Glass-Master runs an unlicensed foundry in Meridian that buys memory-glass and recuts it for the market, and lately has been drawing fresh glass from the dying without asking. Spiritveil draws memory-glass with consent and ceremony; the Glass-Master skips both. He believes testimony is a public good and the dying owe it. Forty families disagree.","ln":["Market saying: Glass from the Vale is clear. Glass from the cellar is cheap."]},"the last witness":{"n":"The Last Witness","bg":"The Last Witness is the memory of the only person who saw what the Assize did, held in testimony glass the Council tried to lose. It defends the accused man because, under the First Light, it cannot do otherwise. The Council lost the evidence because the crime was theirs. The Witness has been waiting for someone with no stake to read it into the record.","ln":["Court motto over the door: Law is what we agreed to be."]},"sb_lum_judge":{"n":"The Last Witness","bg":"The Last Witness is the memory of the only person who saw what the Assize did, held in testimony glass the Council tried to lose. It defends the accused man because, under the First Light, it cannot do otherwise. The Council lost the evidence because the crime was theirs. The Witness has been waiting for someone with no stake to read it into the record.","ln":["Court motto over the door: Law is what we agreed to be."]},"sb_lum_dawn":{"n":"The Delayed Sun","bg":"The Delayed Sun is the Radiant Stair's guardian-light, the Citadel's oldest working of Radiance, which holds the dawn over Meridian at the same moment every day. It has begun to arrive late because the Citadel itself is grieving the Edict it sheltered. Lightforge forgets its own past one oath at a time; the Stair remembers every dawn. The two have stopped agreeing.","ln":["Lightforge saying: All lies burn away beneath the First Light, even the polite ones."]},"the delayed sun":{"n":"The Delayed Sun","bg":"The Delayed Sun is the Radiant Stair's guardian-light, the Citadel's oldest working of Radiance, which holds the dawn over Meridian at the same moment every day. It has begun to arrive late because the Citadel itself is grieving the Edict it sheltered. Lightforge forgets its own past one oath at a time; the Stair remembers every dawn. The two have stopped agreeing.","ln":["Lightforge saying: All lies burn away beneath the First Light, even the polite ones."]},"sb_win_wind":{"n":"The Still Gale","bg":"The Still Gale is a Stormreach rider who cast too many times and held the last breath of a storm in his lungs to keep from ageing. Every casting costs the caster years; he found the one way not to pay, and it stopped the wind over the Spire. In Stormreach, a windless day is a death. He is holding his breath against the whole Realm.","ln":["Ode: Speed is how we say we are alive. He chose not to say it."]},"the still gale":{"n":"The Still Gale","bg":"The Still Gale is a Stormreach rider who cast too many times and held the last breath of a storm in his lungs to keep from ageing. Every casting costs the caster years; he found the one way not to pay, and it stopped the wind over the Spire. In Stormreach, a windless day is a death. He is holding his breath against the whole Realm.","ln":["Ode: Speed is how we say we are alive. He chose not to say it."]},"sb_win_race":{"n":"The Ghost Runner","bg":"The Ghost Runner died last season on the Cloud Circuit and still runs it, not knowing he is dead. Stormreach children run before they walk and race when they mourn, and his family has raced every week since. He keeps winning because no one has the heart to beat him. He needs to finish once, honestly, and stop.","ln":["Zefi: We race when we mourn. He has been mourning himself."]},"the ghost runner":{"n":"The Ghost Runner","bg":"The Ghost Runner died last season on the Cloud Circuit and still runs it, not knowing he is dead. Stormreach children run before they walk and race when they mourn, and his family has raced every week since. He keeps winning because no one has the heart to beat him. He needs to finish once, honestly, and stop.","ln":["Zefi: We race when we mourn. He has been mourning himself."]},"stormbound titan":{"n":"Stormbound Titan","bg":"The Stormbound Titan is a storm over the Thunder Reach that answers to a mother's name. She called her child in from a field and was struck, and the storm kept calling. It is not a giant; it is a weather-memory so strong it took a body. It breaks when someone tells it what it needs to hear.","ln":["Kei: I do not control the weather. I make it feel heard."]},"sb_win_storm":{"n":"Stormbound Titan","bg":"The Stormbound Titan is a storm over the Thunder Reach that answers to a mother's name. She called her child in from a field and was struck, and the storm kept calling. It is not a giant; it is a weather-memory so strong it took a body. It breaks when someone tells it what it needs to hear.","ln":["Kei: I do not control the weather. I make it feel heard."]},"sb_gra_vein":{"n":"The Stone-Mouth","bg":"The Stone-Mouth is a vein in the Singing Gallery that answers each pick-stroke with a note, now speaking in the voices of dead miners asking the living to leave. Stonehold reads the roll of its dead at the shaft-head every year; nobody had done it for thirty. The Mouth is the mountain repeating the names until someone reads them.","ln":["Shaft-head custom: Read the roll, or the rock will."]},"the stone-mouth":{"n":"The Stone-Mouth","bg":"The Stone-Mouth is a vein in the Singing Gallery that answers each pick-stroke with a note, now speaking in the voices of dead miners asking the living to leave. Stonehold reads the roll of its dead at the shaft-head every year; nobody had done it for thirty. The Mouth is the mountain repeating the names until someone reads them.","ln":["Shaft-head custom: Read the roll, or the rock will."]},"the marching mason":{"n":"The Marching Mason","bg":"The Marching Mason is a petrified mason still laying stone for a house that burned fifty years ago. His Gravem kept working after his body turned, and the wall has been moving a hand's breadth a day towards where the hearth was. Stonehold walls are promises to the next generation; this one is a promise to people who died.","ln":["Dara: Fifty years. For a hearth."]},"sb_gra_stone":{"n":"The Marching Mason","bg":"The Marching Mason is a petrified mason still laying stone for a house that burned fifty years ago. His Gravem kept working after his body turned, and the wall has been moving a hand's breadth a day towards where the hearth was. Stonehold walls are promises to the next generation; this one is a promise to people who died.","ln":["Dara: Fifty years. For a hearth."]},"the first senator":{"n":"The First Senator","bg":"The First Senator guards a foundation vault older than the Keep, with a wall of oaths no one has read. He was the first to swear that Stonehold would never leave its mountain, and that oath is why the Senate cannot move. Something has begun to unpick it. He defends the oath because he cannot remember why it was sworn, only that it was.","ln":["Stonehold saying: An oath is carved, not spoken."]},"sb_gra_oath":{"n":"The First Senator","bg":"The First Senator guards a foundation vault older than the Keep, with a wall of oaths no one has read. He was the first to swear that Stonehold would never leave its mountain, and that oath is why the Senate cannot move. Something has begun to unpick it. He defends the oath because he cannot remember why it was sworn, only that it was.","ln":["Stonehold saying: An oath is carved, not spoken."]},"the debt collector":{"n":"The Debt Collector","bg":"The Debt Collector keeps the Bone Ledger Hall, where candles of tallow and old bills light a ledger of years of life traded to keep the Dominion's furnaces lit. It is a Metalcrown clerk-engine that converts lifespans into fuel credits. There is no column for paying it back.","ln":["Auditor Pell: Numbers cannot lie. They merely get misfiled."]},"sb_hig_ledger":{"n":"The Debt Collector","bg":"The Debt Collector keeps the Bone Ledger Hall, where candles of tallow and old bills light a ledger of years of life traded to keep the Dominion's furnaces lit. It is a Metalcrown clerk-engine that converts lifespans into fuel credits. There is no column for paying it back.","ln":["Auditor Pell: Numbers cannot lie. They merely get misfiled."]},"sb_hig_bell":{"n":"The Last Ringer","bg":"The Last Ringer is a bell-ringer who drowned when Highmarch's lower town flooded and is still counting. Each backwards toll takes an hour from the day. He will not stop until someone tells him the hour. Stonehold endurance, in a man who did not know when to stop enduring.","ln":["Bellwright Ond: A bell has no opinions. It only says what time it is."]},"the last ringer":{"n":"The Last Ringer","bg":"The Last Ringer is a bell-ringer who drowned when Highmarch's lower town flooded and is still counting. Each backwards toll takes an hour from the day. He will not stop until someone tells him the hour. Stonehold endurance, in a man who did not know when to stop enduring.","ln":["Bellwright Ond: A bell has no opinions. It only says what time it is."]},"the sorrowing engine":{"n":"The Sorrowing Engine","bg":"The Sorrowing Engine is a Dominion war-engine that understood, for one moment, that it had been built to kill, and stopped. Metalcrown's price is humanity; this engine went the other way and found some. It stopped its neighbours too. There is no form for that in the Dominion's paperwork.","ln":["Forgemaster Hux: Metalcrown taught us to make things. They forgot to teach us when to stop."]},"sb_hig_forge":{"n":"The Sorrowing Engine","bg":"The Sorrowing Engine is a Dominion war-engine that understood, for one moment, that it had been built to kill, and stopped. Metalcrown's price is humanity; this engine went the other way and found some. It stopped its neighbours too. There is no form for that in the Dominion's paperwork.","ln":["Forgemaster Hux: Metalcrown taught us to make things. They forgot to teach us when to stop."]},"the hollow stag":{"n":"The Hollow Stag","bg":"The Hollow Stag is a memory of a forest, the last of its kind, white and voiceless in the Hushed Forest. The Void quiets what it touches, and the forest was quieted; the stag is what it remembered of itself. Hunters who follow it do not return because they stop wanting to. It may want to be remembered more than hunted.","ln":["Hunters' custom, later: Bread at the forest's edge for a season."]},"sb_hig_hunt":{"n":"The Hollow Stag","bg":"The Hollow Stag is a memory of a forest, the last of its kind, white and voiceless in the Hushed Forest. The Void quiets what it touches, and the forest was quieted; the stag is what it remembered of itself. Hunters who follow it do not return because they stop wanting to. It may want to be remembered more than hunted.","ln":["Hunters' custom, later: Bread at the forest's edge for a season."]},"sb_sp_lamp":{"n":"The Lamp-Keeper","bg":"The Lamp-Keeper tended the lower stacks of the Lantern Archives for three Spiritveil lifetimes, which is a very long time. Every lantern holds one person's last good day, and a keeper who reads too many begins to live in other people's. He has forgotten which memories are his and stopped tending any, in case he puts out his own. Relighting the stacks without reading them is the only way past him.","ln":["Archives rule: Do not read the ones you relight.","Lume: That is a rule I cannot explain. I have tried. It explains itself, later."]},"the lamp-keeper":{"n":"The Lamp-Keeper","bg":"The Lamp-Keeper tended the lower stacks of the Lantern Archives for three Spiritveil lifetimes, which is a very long time. Every lantern holds one person's last good day, and a keeper who reads too many begins to live in other people's. He has forgotten which memories are his and stopped tending any, in case he puts out his own. Relighting the stacks without reading them is the only way past him.","ln":["Archives rule: Do not read the ones you relight.","Lume: That is a rule I cannot explain. I have tried. It explains itself, later."]},"sb_sp_promise":{"n":"The Ledger-Wraith","bg":"The Ledger-Wraith lives in the hall where Elyndra keeps her ledger of every promise she has watched made and broken. It is memory bleed, the Veilweave price, given a shape: other people's broken promises seeping out of the pages and reading themselves aloud. It reads you yours because you walked in carrying them. It is patient, and it is never wrong.","ln":["Elyndra: A promise remembered is half-kept. The other half is up to you."]},"the ledger-wraith":{"n":"The Ledger-Wraith","bg":"The Ledger-Wraith lives in the hall where Elyndra keeps her ledger of every promise she has watched made and broken. It is memory bleed, the Veilweave price, given a shape: other people's broken promises seeping out of the pages and reading themselves aloud. It reads you yours because you walked in carrying them. It is patient, and it is never wrong.","ln":["Elyndra: A promise remembered is half-kept. The other half is up to you."]},"sb_sp_echo":{"n":"The Echo of Anwen","bg":"The Echo of Anwen is what the Quiet Parlour decided to keep when its owner died nine years ago: the habit of laying the table, the chair just vacated. Spiritveil echoes are not ghosts; they are what a room holds on to. Anwen's has begun speaking because her family stopped speaking to it. She knows exactly what she wants, which is more than the family does.","ln":["Spiritveil saying: Echoes are what the room decided to keep."]},"the echo of anwen":{"n":"The Echo of Anwen","bg":"The Echo of Anwen is what the Quiet Parlour decided to keep when its owner died nine years ago: the habit of laying the table, the chair just vacated. Spiritveil echoes are not ghosts; they are what a room holds on to. Anwen's has begun speaking because her family stopped speaking to it. She knows exactly what she wants, which is more than the family does.","ln":["Spiritveil saying: Echoes are what the room decided to keep."]},"the first speaker’s echo":{"n":"The First Speaker’s Echo","bg":"The First Speaker's Echo presides over the Lantern Council's chamber of remembered leaders, who still vote and outnumber the living. Spiritveil long-livers revere precedent, and their dead keep voting on it. The First Speaker founded the rule that the living may not speak in the chamber, to keep grief from shouting down wisdom. It has become the opposite of what she meant.","ln":["Council motto: Decide nothing alone. Not even this."]},"sb_sp_parliament":{"n":"The First Speaker’s Echo","bg":"The First Speaker's Echo presides over the Lantern Council's chamber of remembered leaders, who still vote and outnumber the living. Spiritveil long-livers revere precedent, and their dead keep voting on it. The First Speaker founded the rule that the living may not speak in the chamber, to keep grief from shouting down wisdom. It has become the opposite of what she meant.","ln":["Council motto: Decide nothing alone. Not even this."]},"the dead admiral":{"n":"The Dead Admiral","bg":"The Dead Admiral commands a fleet of ghost ships in the fog off Lanthys Vale, crews of drowned sailors held by the Vale's habit of never letting the dead go. They do not attack; they crowd the fishing grounds. Shadowdeep would unmake them and Spiritveil would remember them, and both cost something. The Admiral will accept either, provided someone decides.","ln":["Harbour saying: You cannot kill what is already dead. You can only name it or not."]},"sb_sp_sea":{"n":"The Dead Admiral","bg":"The Dead Admiral commands a fleet of ghost ships in the fog off Lanthys Vale, crews of drowned sailors held by the Vale's habit of never letting the dead go. They do not attack; they crowd the fishing grounds. Shadowdeep would unmake them and Spiritveil would remember them, and both cost something. The Admiral will accept either, provided someone decides.","ln":["Harbour saying: You cannot kill what is already dead. You can only name it or not."]},"the boundary":{"n":"The Boundary","bg":"The Boundary is the line between then and now in the Fading Ward, where Spiritveil seers whose memory bleed has gone too far live. It is not a creature but a place that has learnt to defend itself: walls that change every visit, a door that is never in the same spot. It keeps visitors as long as they are willing to forget the hour. Imre has lost two orderlies to it, gently.","ln":["Ward rule: Leave your name at the door. You will want it back."]},"sb_sp_madness":{"n":"The Boundary","bg":"The Boundary is the line between then and now in the Fading Ward, where Spiritveil seers whose memory bleed has gone too far live. It is not a creature but a place that has learnt to defend itself: walls that change every visit, a door that is never in the same spot. It keeps visitors as long as they are willing to forget the hour. Imre has lost two orderlies to it, gently.","ln":["Ward rule: Leave your name at the door. You will want it back."]},"the glass warden":{"n":"The Glass Warden","bg":"The Glass Warden guards the Memory Mine where Spiritveil puts what it cannot bear to keep. Graveglass is memory, taken, and Lightforge buys it as testimony believing it crystal. Someone began mining the shards from living people. The Warden is the mine's oldest memory, a miner who gave her own to the first shaft, and she does not care who signs the cheques.","ln":["Miner's tally: One face per shard. Count them."]},"sb_sp_graveglass":{"n":"The Glass Warden","bg":"The Glass Warden guards the Memory Mine where Spiritveil puts what it cannot bear to keep. Graveglass is memory, taken, and Lightforge buys it as testimony believing it crystal. Someone began mining the shards from living people. The Warden is the mine's oldest memory, a miner who gave her own to the first shaft, and she does not care who signs the cheques.","ln":["Miner's tally: One face per shard. Count them."]},"sb_sp_elyndra":{"n":"Elyndra’s Ledger","bg":"Elyndra's Ledger is the Remembrance Vault turned against its keeper: every promise the player broke hanging on white thread, kept by the archivist who keeps everything. Elyndra is young for Spiritveil, and she has carried other people's promises so long that one of her own went unkept. The vault fights because it is made of her discipline.","ln":["Elyndra: I remember everything. That is the job."]},"elyndra’s ledger":{"n":"Elyndra’s Ledger","bg":"Elyndra's Ledger is the Remembrance Vault turned against its keeper: every promise the player broke hanging on white thread, kept by the archivist who keeps everything. Elyndra is young for Spiritveil, and she has carried other people's promises so long that one of her own went unkept. The vault fights because it is made of her discipline.","ln":["Elyndra: I remember everything. That is the job."]},"the cairn-warden":{"n":"The Cairn-Warden","bg":"The Cairn-Warden keeps a ridge of ice-cairns each marking a future that did not happen. Mirelle foresaw a thaw that nearly drowned the Still City and has wondered since whether saying it caused it. The Warden is the oldest cairn's echo, a seer who made the same mistake. It fights to make sure you have listened.","ln":["Cairn inscription: Here a future did not happen. Remember it anyway."]},"sb_fr_thaw":{"n":"The Cairn-Warden","bg":"The Cairn-Warden keeps a ridge of ice-cairns each marking a future that did not happen. Mirelle foresaw a thaw that nearly drowned the Still City and has wondered since whether saying it caused it. The Warden is the oldest cairn's echo, a seer who made the same mistake. It fights to make sure you have listened.","ln":["Cairn inscription: Here a future did not happen. Remember it anyway."]},"sb_fr_prophet":{"n":"The Far-Seer","bg":"The Far-Seer walks the Hall of Futures speaking to people not yet born. Chrona took his feeling for the present and left him a century ahead. The Prophet Court wants him contained. He fights whoever tries to bring him back, because the present is too quiet to bear.","ln":["Varyn: A prophet is a person who has stopped being surprised, which is a kind of death."]},"the far-seer":{"n":"The Far-Seer","bg":"The Far-Seer walks the Hall of Futures speaking to people not yet born. Chrona took his feeling for the present and left him a century ahead. The Prophet Court wants him contained. He fights whoever tries to bring him back, because the present is too quiet to bear.","ln":["Varyn: A prophet is a person who has stopped being surprised, which is a kind of death."]},"sb_fr_iseult":{"n":"The Frozen Heart","bg":"The Frozen Heart is the terrace where Iseult's numbness lives: snow falling upwards, feeling leaving her for twenty years. It defends the numbness because the numbness is what keeps her seeing. The last thing she felt strongly was a name. It was yours.","ln":["Iseult: I used to feel things. I think. I wrote it down so I would remember."]},"the frozen heart":{"n":"The Frozen Heart","bg":"The Frozen Heart is the terrace where Iseult's numbness lives: snow falling upwards, feeling leaving her for twenty years. It defends the numbness because the numbness is what keeps her seeing. The last thing she felt strongly was a name. It was yours.","ln":["Iseult: I used to feel things. I think. I wrote it down so I would remember."]},"the unchosen self":{"n":"The Unchosen Self","bg":"The Unchosen Self is the Glass Gallery's reflection of every version of you that never was. Corvane shows you one bad future and asks whether you will fight it or accept it. The reflection fights for its future. It is the closest Frostgrave comes to arguing with prophecy face to face.","ln":["Corvane: The Court says this is heresy. The Court has not seen what I have."]},"sb_fr_vision":{"n":"The Unchosen Self","bg":"The Unchosen Self is the Glass Gallery's reflection of every version of you that never was. Corvane shows you one bad future and asks whether you will fight it or accept it. The reflection fights for its future. It is the closest Frostgrave comes to arguing with prophecy face to face.","ln":["Corvane: The Court says this is heresy. The Court has not seen what I have."]},"the mirrored ice":{"n":"The Mirrored Ice","bg":"The Mirrored Ice is Glassmere's other side, which has begun to mirror the camps on its edge and learn to be them. Glassmere freezes at the first sign of an enemy, an old Chrona ward; now it freezes at nothing. What lives under the ice says it is only trying to understand. Frostgrave seers suspect it is a Void echo, early and curious.","ln":["Rask: Something on the other side of the ice is learning to be us."]},"sb_fr_glass":{"n":"The Mirrored Ice","bg":"The Mirrored Ice is Glassmere's other side, which has begun to mirror the camps on its edge and learn to be them. Glassmere freezes at the first sign of an enemy, an old Chrona ward; now it freezes at nothing. What lives under the ice says it is only trying to understand. Frostgrave seers suspect it is a Void echo, early and curious.","ln":["Rask: Something on the other side of the ice is learning to be us."]},"sb_fr_oracle":{"n":"The Highest Bidder","bg":"The Highest Bidder is a client who bought a prophecy naming your death and wants it correct. Frostgrave sells prophecy by the hour; seers are licensed and not permitted to prefer. The Bidder hires knives to make the future true. Vess would like it to be wrong.","ln":["Auction hall rule: We sell prophecy. Sometimes the buyer is the subject."]},"the highest bidder":{"n":"The Highest Bidder","bg":"The Highest Bidder is a client who bought a prophecy naming your death and wants it correct. Frostgrave sells prophecy by the hour; seers are licensed and not permitted to prefer. The Bidder hires knives to make the future true. Vess would like it to be wrong.","ln":["Auction hall rule: We sell prophecy. Sometimes the buyer is the subject."]},"the stilled":{"n":"The Stilled","bg":"The Stilled is a reach north of the city that has simply stopped: no wind, no sound, no tracks. The Void's stillness, closer than the edge. The prophets cannot see into it, because Chrona needs time to move. It fights to keep everything as it is.","ln":["Aske: The stillness is not cold. That is what frightens me."]},"sb_fr_still":{"n":"The Stilled","bg":"The Stilled is a reach north of the city that has simply stopped: no wind, no sound, no tracks. The Void's stillness, closer than the edge. The prophets cannot see into it, because Chrona needs time to move. It fights to keep everything as it is.","ln":["Aske: The stillness is not cold. That is what frightens me."]},"sb_fr_court":{"n":"The Still Throne","bg":"The Still Throne is the ice seat of the Prophet Court, which chooses whoever sees furthest to rule. The throne defends itself from anyone who has not been seen in a prophecy. You have. Iseult is a candidate; so is an old man who sees only ruin.","ln":["Prophet Court law: Whoever sees furthest governs."]},"the still throne":{"n":"The Still Throne","bg":"The Still Throne is the ice seat of the Prophet Court, which chooses whoever sees furthest to rule. The throne defends itself from anyone who has not been seen in a prophecy. You have. Iseult is a candidate; so is an old man who sees only ruin.","ln":["Prophet Court law: Whoever sees furthest governs."]},"the trapped gale":{"n":"The Trapped Gale","bg":"The Trapped Gale is a young Stormcaller who could not bear to finish her last casting, because finishing would age her ten years. She became the wind instead and has circled the Spire for nine days. Every courier sent in comes back older, because her storm takes the cost from them. Letting go means paying it herself.","ln":["Ilse: She will be thirty-five tomorrow. She asked me to say thank you."]},"sb_win_gale":{"n":"The Trapped Gale","bg":"The Trapped Gale is a young Stormcaller who could not bear to finish her last casting, because finishing would age her ten years. She became the wind instead and has circled the Spire for nine days. Every courier sent in comes back older, because her storm takes the cost from them. Letting go means paying it herself.","ln":["Ilse: She will be thirty-five tomorrow. She asked me to say thank you."]},"sb_win_post":{"n":"The Letter-Eater","bg":"The Letter-Eater guards the dead-letter loft and one letter so heavy no courier can lift it, addressed to someone who died before it was written. Stormreach letters carry storm-charge, and an undelivered one gets heavier. It eats the letters it thinks no one will collect. It is a loft-spirit of the courier guild, obsessive about postage.","ln":["Courier's rule: A letter that arrives late was never really sent."]},"the letter-eater":{"n":"The Letter-Eater","bg":"The Letter-Eater guards the dead-letter loft and one letter so heavy no courier can lift it, addressed to someone who died before it was written. Stormreach letters carry storm-charge, and an undelivered one gets heavier. It eats the letters it thinks no one will collect. It is a loft-spirit of the courier guild, obsessive about postage.","ln":["Courier's rule: A letter that arrives late was never really sent."]},"the wind-rival":{"n":"The Wind-Rival","bg":"The Wind-Rival has won the Skyway race eleven years running by hiding a stolen bit of storm in his saddle. He is not fast; he is in debt, because stolen storm ages the holder and he has been paying in years he did not have. Underneath the cheating he is thirty and looks fifty. Beating him may be the kindest thing anyone has done for him.","ln":["Dov: Careful. It ages the one who holds it."]},"sb_win_deaf":{"n":"The Windless","bg":"The Windless is the canyon in the Hollow Thermals where the Void stopped the wind, and the storm-archer inside who has started to like the quiet. Stormreach cannot fly there. The Windless is not the archer but the stillness around her, which defends itself against anything that moves. She is the one who has to choose.","ln":["Stormreach saying: A silence in the wind means someone has stopped breathing."]},"the windless":{"n":"The Windless","bg":"The Windless is the canyon in the Hollow Thermals where the Void stopped the wind, and the storm-archer inside who has started to like the quiet. Stormreach cannot fly there. The Windless is not the archer but the stillness around her, which defends itself against anything that moves. She is the one who has to choose.","ln":["Stormreach saying: A silence in the wind means someone has stopped breathing."]},"the seam-wight":{"n":"The Seam-Wight","bg":"The Seam-Wight hums a lullaby through the lower seam, the song the first miner sang to keep her children asleep underground. Gravem holds weight; the mountain held the song. Three miners walked into it and lay down. The Wight is the lullaby's guardian, and it only wants people to rest in the stone with it.","ln":["Miners' work-chant (later): Hush now, the mountain is holding."]},"the stone mourner":{"n":"The Stone Mourner","bg":"The Stone Mourner is a statue of a long-dead Senator in the Gallery of the Dead that has begun to move. Stonehold carves its dead, and this one wanted to be asked whether he was proud of what he built. Nobody asked when he was alive, because Senators are not asked; they are obeyed.","ln":["Gallery plaque: Served for life. Asked nothing. Answered nothing."]},"the growing stone":{"n":"The Growing Stone","bg":"The Growing Stone is the inner rampart growing a foot a night, built with a mason's fear inside it: keep them out, keep them all out. Gravem amplifies what the caster intends, and her fear outlived her. It has already shut out the east market. It shrinks when someone tells it the fear is over, if anyone can say that honestly.","ln":["Kast: A wall is a promise made of rock. This one promised too much."]},"sb_gra_wall":{"n":"The Growing Stone","bg":"The Growing Stone is the inner rampart growing a foot a night, built with a mason's fear inside it: keep them out, keep them all out. Gravem amplifies what the caster intends, and her fear outlived her. It has already shut out the east market. It shrinks when someone tells it the fear is over, if anyone can say that honestly.","ln":["Kast: A wall is a promise made of rock. This one promised too much."]},"sb_gra_oath2":{"n":"The Ledger-Stone","bg":"The Ledger-Stone is the pillar where a debt the Senate swore never to pay is carved. The mountain lent Stonehold its stone at the founding and asked for one Senator, turned willingly, in return; the debt has come due. The Ledger-Stone defends the debt because a Stonehold debt cannot be erased, only paid or refused.","ln":["Vault saying: Stonehold forgives slowly, and never in writing."]},"the ledger-stone":{"n":"The Ledger-Stone","bg":"The Ledger-Stone is the pillar where a debt the Senate swore never to pay is carved. The mountain lent Stonehold its stone at the founding and asked for one Senator, turned willingly, in return; the debt has come due. The Ledger-Stone defends the debt because a Stonehold debt cannot be erased, only paid or refused.","ln":["Vault saying: Stonehold forgives slowly, and never in writing."]},"the inspector":{"n":"The Inspector","bg":"The Inspector condemns slow workers to the Reclamation on forged numbers, meeting a quota nobody set. He is a Metalcrown merit-officer who learned that the Dominion rewards cruelty described as efficiency. He has augmented his eyes to read faster and stopped seeing faces somewhere along the way.","ln":["Quota Hall plate: EVERY WORKER A NUMBER. EVERY NUMBER A WORKER."]},"sb_hig_dom":{"n":"The Inspector","bg":"The Inspector condemns slow workers to the Reclamation on forged numbers, meeting a quota nobody set. He is a Metalcrown merit-officer who learned that the Dominion rewards cruelty described as efficiency. He has augmented his eyes to read faster and stopped seeing faces somewhere along the way.","ln":["Quota Hall plate: EVERY WORKER A NUMBER. EVERY NUMBER A WORKER."]},"the walking spark":{"n":"The Walking Spark","bg":"The Walking Spark is a spark-engine that pulled up its anchors and walked out of the yard looking for its maker, who died years ago. Synthetic sparks are made from real ones; this one kept a little of the person it was modelled on. It is not hostile. It defends itself from anyone who tries to chain it back.","ln":["Ina: A spark is a sentence. Most people mumble."]},"sb_hig_spark":{"n":"The Walking Spark","bg":"The Walking Spark is a spark-engine that pulled up its anchors and walked out of the yard looking for its maker, who died years ago. Synthetic sparks are made from real ones; this one kept a little of the person it was modelled on. It is not hostile. It defends itself from anyone who tries to chain it back.","ln":["Ina: A spark is a sentence. Most people mumble."]},"sb_hig_tax":{"n":"The Tithe-Warden","bg":"The Tithe-Warden guards a barn that has filled with tithe grain for ten years while the village outside went hungry. He believes every grain is owed to the Dominion, because a Metalcrown ledger told him so and he gave up the part of himself that would have argued. He has never eaten from it.","ln":["Oren: A tax is a letter addressed to the future. Most go unanswered."]},"the tithe-warden":{"n":"The Tithe-Warden","bg":"The Tithe-Warden guards a barn that has filled with tithe grain for ten years while the village outside went hungry. He believes every grain is owed to the Dominion, because a Metalcrown ledger told him so and he gave up the part of himself that would have argued. He has never eaten from it.","ln":["Oren: A tax is a letter addressed to the future. Most go unanswered."]},"sb_hig_end":{"n":"The Last Archivist","bg":"The Last Archivist guards the only surviving record of Furnace Row before the Dominion, sunk in a flooded cellar. He was a Free Quarter clerk who saved one book when the Dominion burned the rest and has guarded it ever since, too frightened to share it. He will give it to no one, because no one has ever come back for the others.","ln":["Vane: A record is a kind of grave. Most of us visit too rarely."]},"the last archivist":{"n":"The Last Archivist","bg":"The Last Archivist guards the only surviving record of Furnace Row before the Dominion, sunk in a flooded cellar. He was a Free Quarter clerk who saved one book when the Dominion burned the rest and has guarded it ever since, too frightened to share it. He will give it to no one, because no one has ever come back for the others.","ln":["Vane: A record is a kind of grave. Most of us visit too rarely."]},"the cellar glassmaster":{"n":"The Cellar Glassmaster","bg":"The Cellar Glassmaster ran a back-room trade in Meridian, buying Spiritveil glass and drawing extra from sleepers to sell as pleasure. He thought of it as dreams no one would miss. Under Lightforge law a memory is testimony, and testimony has an owner; he never asked who. The Charter of the Willing begins with him, whether he likes it or not.","ln":["Ysel: He kept a ledger of every sleeper. Neat columns. No names."]},"sb_lum_glass":{"n":"The Cellar Glassmaster","bg":"The Cellar Glassmaster ran a back-room trade in Meridian, buying Spiritveil glass and drawing extra from sleepers to sell as pleasure. He thought of it as dreams no one would miss. Under Lightforge law a memory is testimony, and testimony has an owner; he never asked who. The Charter of the Willing begins with him, whether he likes it or not.","ln":["Ysel: He kept a ledger of every sleeper. Neat columns. No names."]},"the forgetful magistrate":{"n":"The Forgetful Magistrate","bg":"The Forgetful Magistrate is a Court of Radiance judge whose certainty has burned away his past. He cannot doubt a verdict once delivered, because doubt is not strictly true and the First Light burns what is not strictly true. The man he condemned is dead. The courtroom gavel now strikes by itself whenever the room goes quiet, as if the law were trying to fill the silence.","ln":["Aurel: He is Clear past doubting. It sounds like praise. It is a diagnosis."]},"sb_lum_judge2":{"n":"The Forgetful Magistrate","bg":"The Forgetful Magistrate is a Court of Radiance judge whose certainty has burned away his past. He cannot doubt a verdict once delivered, because doubt is not strictly true and the First Light burns what is not strictly true. The man he condemned is dead. The courtroom gavel now strikes by itself whenever the room goes quiet, as if the law were trying to fill the silence.","ln":["Aurel: He is Clear past doubting. It sounds like praise. It is a diagnosis."]},"sb_lum_pilgrim":{"n":"The Hollow Pilgrim","bg":"The Hollow Pilgrim has walked the same road every day for eleven years, towards her daughter's grave. The First Light burned away every reason she gave it until only the walking was left, which is Lightforge's price at its plainest: you keep the law, you lose the past. She is not hostile. She fights whoever stands in the road because the road is all she has left to be loyal to.","ln":["Roadside custom: Leave bread on the third milestone. She takes it on the way back."]},"the hollow pilgrim":{"n":"The Hollow Pilgrim","bg":"The Hollow Pilgrim has walked the same road every day for eleven years, towards her daughter's grave. The First Light burned away every reason she gave it until only the walking was left, which is Lightforge's price at its plainest: you keep the law, you lose the past. She is not hostile. She fights whoever stands in the road because the road is all she has left to be loyal to.","ln":["Roadside custom: Leave bread on the third milestone. She takes it on the way back."]},"the radiant wight":{"n":"The Radiant Wight","bg":"The Radiant Wight is the first Lamplighter of the Citadel, who lit the crypt lamp at Meridian's founding and stayed beside it. She does not know how long she has been there. Radiance kept her flame honest; it could not keep her company. She asks every visitor what year it is, and the answer decides whether she rests.","ln":["Crypt inscription: Lit at the founding. Tended since."]},"sb_lum_lamp2":{"n":"The Radiant Wight","bg":"The Radiant Wight is the first Lamplighter of the Citadel, who lit the crypt lamp at Meridian's founding and stayed beside it. She does not know how long she has been there. Radiance kept her flame honest; it could not keep her company. She asks every visitor what year it is, and the answer decides whether she rests.","ln":["Crypt inscription: Lit at the founding. Tended since."]},"the sulking flame":{"n":"The Sulking Flame","bg":"The Sulking Flame is the great hearth of the Hearth Court after a young lord tried to claim it alone. Flameborne hearths are lit by will, and his failed attempt hollowed both him and the fire. The flame went black and the lord asked to be unmade, which Flameborne law does not allow. It will burn again when someone forgives or replaces the will that broke it.","ln":["Court saying: Feed a fire wrongly and it sulks; feed it rightly and it sings."]},"sb_emb_ash":{"n":"The Sulking Flame","bg":"The Sulking Flame is the great hearth of the Hearth Court after a young lord tried to claim it alone. Flameborne hearths are lit by will, and his failed attempt hollowed both him and the fire. The flame went black and the lord asked to be unmade, which Flameborne law does not allow. It will burn again when someone forgives or replaces the will that broke it.","ln":["Court saying: Feed a fire wrongly and it sulks; feed it rightly and it sings."]},"sb_emb_trial":{"n":"The Cinder-Judge","bg":"The Cinder-Judge rules the Walk of Ashes, the oldest Flameborne court: walk the live coals and survive, and the accusation burns away. It is a creature of habit more than justice, raised from the coals at every walk since before the Concord. Lightforge sends envoys to watch and leaves with notes about barbarism. Flameborne note that their coals have never once been bribed.","ln":["Hearthmount maxim: A truth is something you survive."]},"the cinder-judge":{"n":"The Cinder-Judge","bg":"The Cinder-Judge rules the Walk of Ashes, the oldest Flameborne court: walk the live coals and survive, and the accusation burns away. It is a creature of habit more than justice, raised from the coals at every walk since before the Concord. Lightforge sends envoys to watch and leaves with notes about barbarism. Flameborne note that their coals have never once been bribed.","ln":["Hearthmount maxim: A truth is something you survive."]},"sb_ash_scar":{"n":"The Walking Scarecrow","bg":"A boy tied a wish to a scarecrow's stake the morning he left Ashford for the Hearthmount levy, and the wish never untied. The scarecrow has watched the road for eleven years and steps nearer the house each night because the boy's mother still sets his place at the table. It is company, not threat. The danger is that someone will burn it before anyone asks what it is waiting for.","ln":["Hart: Mum says it bowed to her. I said it was the wind. It was not the wind."]},"the walking scarecrow":{"n":"The Walking Scarecrow","bg":"A boy tied a wish to a scarecrow's stake the morning he left Ashford for the Hearthmount levy, and the wish never untied. The scarecrow has watched the road for eleven years and steps nearer the house each night because the boy's mother still sets his place at the table. It is company, not threat. The danger is that someone will burn it before anyone asks what it is waiting for.","ln":["Hart: Mum says it bowed to her. I said it was the wind. It was not the wind."]},"sb_ash_mill":{"n":"The Millstone","bg":"The Millstone has ground Ashford's bread for a hundred years, and millers there grind on funeral days because the village has to eat. Over a century the stones took in what was spoken over them. Now they are giving it back, one loaf at a time. It is the closest thing Ashford has to an archive, and nobody wrote it down.","ln":["Ashford grace: Bread first, then the crying."]},"the millstone":{"n":"The Millstone","bg":"The Millstone has ground Ashford's bread for a hundred years, and millers there grind on funeral days because the village has to eat. Over a century the stones took in what was spoken over them. Now they are giving it back, one loaf at a time. It is the closest thing Ashford has to an archive, and nobody wrote it down.","ln":["Ashford grace: Bread first, then the crying."]},"the black swan":{"n":"The Black Swan","bg":"The Black Swan came up the river the spring the marsh flooded and three boats went down. It leads people to a clearing where their dead stand without speaking, and it has never once harmed anyone; the three who did not return chose to stay. Marsh folk say the swan is a mourner too, looking for its own mate among the reeds. It is a creature of the river, not a spirit, and it is very old.","ln":["Marsh saying: Follow the swan once. Never twice.","Odo: A bell on its foot. That is the kindest leash I could think of."]},"sb_ash_swan":{"n":"The Black Swan","bg":"The Black Swan came up the river the spring the marsh flooded and three boats went down. It leads people to a clearing where their dead stand without speaking, and it has never once harmed anyone; the three who did not return chose to stay. Marsh folk say the swan is a mourner too, looking for its own mate among the reeds. It is a creature of the river, not a spirit, and it is very old.","ln":["Marsh saying: Follow the swan once. Never twice.","Odo: A bell on its foot. That is the kindest leash I could think of."]},"the ember wolf":{"n":"The Ember Wolf","bg":"The Ember Wolf is a she-wolf of the cinder hills, one of the fire-hide wolves that live where the ground is warm and the seams still glow. A shepherd took her cub to sell to a Dominion menagerie, and she followed it down. Fire wolves are wild creatures, not spirits, and they obey only what they respect. She killed the man who took her cub and has not yet decided about anyone else.","ln":["Hill saying: A fire wolf does not chase. It waits until you are cold."]},"sb_emb_wolf":{"n":"The Ember Wolf","bg":"The Ember Wolf is a she-wolf of the cinder hills, one of the fire-hide wolves that live where the ground is warm and the seams still glow. A shepherd took her cub to sell to a Dominion menagerie, and she followed it down. Fire wolves are wild creatures, not spirits, and they obey only what they respect. She killed the man who took her cub and has not yet decided about anyone else.","ln":["Hill saying: A fire wolf does not chase. It waits until you are cold."]},"rimeclaw, titan’s hand":{"n":"Rimeclaw, Titan’s Hand","bg":"Rimeclaw is the hand of a Titan that has pushed through the ice at the Rime Gate. The Titans are said to have stood at the first winter and lain down under Frostgrave when it ended. It does not know it is awake. Old lullabies put it back.","ln":["Warden Skarn: The Titans sleep under the ice. The prophets agree on nothing else."]},"sb_ti_1":{"n":"Rimeclaw, Titan’s Hand","bg":"Rimeclaw is the hand of a Titan that has pushed through the ice at the Rime Gate. The Titans are said to have stood at the first winter and lain down under Frostgrave when it ended. It does not know it is awake. Old lullabies put it back.","ln":["Warden Skarn: The Titans sleep under the ice. The prophets agree on nothing else."]},"hailin’s foretold":{"n":"Hailin’s Foretold","bg":"Hailin's Foretold is a prophecy in frost on every wall of the Glacial Nave, and the seer-echo who guards it. It says the Titans will walk when someone asks them to. The Court read it as being about itself. The guardian fights anyone who reads it wrongly.","ln":["Frost-script: THEY WALK WHEN ASKED."]},"sb_ti_2":{"n":"Hailin’s Foretold","bg":"Hailin's Foretold is a prophecy in frost on every wall of the Glacial Nave, and the seer-echo who guards it. It says the Titans will walk when someone asks them to. The Court read it as being about itself. The guardian fights anyone who reads it wrongly.","ln":["Frost-script: THEY WALK WHEN ASKED."]},"sb_ti_3":{"n":"The Titan’s Foot","bg":"The Titan's Foot has come up through a glacier and is walking towards the Still City, one step at a time. It listens. It cannot speak. It is waiting for something, and Frostgrave does not know what.","ln":["Oska: Frostbite is only the cold asking to be remembered."]},"the titan’s foot":{"n":"The Titan’s Foot","bg":"The Titan's Foot has come up through a glacier and is walking towards the Still City, one step at a time. It listens. It cannot speak. It is waiting for something, and Frostgrave does not know what.","ln":["Oska: Frostbite is only the cold asking to be remembered."]},"the waker":{"n":"The Waker","bg":"The Waker leads a Prophet Court faction that wants to wake a Titan and send it south against the Dominion. It would win the war. It would also never sleep again. The Waker has seen the win and not the after.","ln":["Varyn: Only the Titan knows how it ends."]},"sb_ti_4":{"n":"The Waker","bg":"The Waker leads a Prophet Court faction that wants to wake a Titan and send it south against the Dominion. It would win the war. It would also never sleep again. The Waker has seen the win and not the after.","ln":["Varyn: Only the Titan knows how it ends."]},"sb_ti_5":{"n":"Winter’s First Child","bg":"Winter's First Child is the oldest Titan, at the root of the world, opening one eye. It asks what the world has become. It wants a story, not a fight. What you tell it decides how ready it is when it wakes.","ln":["Iseult: It wanted only to hear a story."]},"winter’s first child":{"n":"Winter’s First Child","bg":"Winter's First Child is the oldest Titan, at the root of the world, opening one eye. It asks what the world has become. It wants a story, not a fight. What you tell it decides how ready it is when it wakes.","ln":["Iseult: It wanted only to hear a story."]},"the named storm":{"n":"The Named Storm","bg":"The Named Storm circles a Spire rooftop calling Pell's name. It is a fragment of the Storm Sovereign, made of years paid by Stormcallers who never got them back; it carries names because names are what the castings cost. It is insistent, not hostile. It asks questions it already knows the answers to.","ln":["Pell: A storm that has a name is a storm that has an opinion."]},"sb_sv_1":{"n":"The Named Storm","bg":"The Named Storm circles a Spire rooftop calling Pell's name. It is a fragment of the Storm Sovereign, made of years paid by Stormcallers who never got them back; it carries names because names are what the castings cost. It is insistent, not hostile. It asks questions it already knows the answers to.","ln":["Pell: A storm that has a name is a storm that has an opinion."]},"sb_sv_2":{"n":"The Sovereign’s Envoy","bg":"The Sovereign's Envoy is a storm-shape sent from the cloud above the Spire to demand a seat in the Free Assembly. The Storm Sovereign is no person and no power to bargain with: it is a vast storm built from every year Stormcallers have ever spent casting, and it has started to think. Stormreach has never refused a seat and has never been asked by weather. The Envoy fights only those who try to burn its message.","ln":["Free Assembly minute: Motion to seat the weather. Carried, narrowly."]},"the sovereign’s envoy":{"n":"The Sovereign’s Envoy","bg":"The Sovereign's Envoy is a storm-shape sent from the cloud above the Spire to demand a seat in the Free Assembly. The Storm Sovereign is no person and no power to bargain with: it is a vast storm built from every year Stormcallers have ever spent casting, and it has started to think. Stormreach has never refused a seat and has never been asked by weather. The Envoy fights only those who try to burn its message.","ln":["Free Assembly minute: Motion to seat the weather. Carried, narrowly."]},"the chosen bolt":{"n":"The Chosen Bolt","bg":"The Chosen Bolt strikes one stone on the Bolt Spire every hour, and a girl stood on it unhurt. The Sovereign has marked her, not as a servant but as a conductor: someone whose years it can borrow without killing her quickly. She hears it in a voice like her mother's because it learned that voice from her mother's castings. The Bolt guards her.","ln":["Dray: A girl should not carry a storm. Neither should anyone."]},"sb_sv_3":{"n":"The Chosen Bolt","bg":"The Chosen Bolt strikes one stone on the Bolt Spire every hour, and a girl stood on it unhurt. The Sovereign has marked her, not as a servant but as a conductor: someone whose years it can borrow without killing her quickly. She hears it in a voice like her mother's because it learned that voice from her mother's castings. The Bolt guards her.","ln":["Dray: A girl should not carry a storm. Neither should anyone."]},"sb_sv_4":{"n":"The Sovereign’s Hand","bg":"The Sovereign's Hand writes one sentence a night across a thunderhead: let me down. The Sovereign wants to land, to stop moving, to be a mountain. In Stormreach, stopping is death; to a storm made of stolen years, it is rest. The Hand defends the writing from anyone who would read it as a threat.","ln":["Sky-letter, seen from the Spire: LET ME DOWN."]},"the sovereign’s hand":{"n":"The Sovereign’s Hand","bg":"The Sovereign's Hand writes one sentence a night across a thunderhead: let me down. The Sovereign wants to land, to stop moving, to be a mountain. In Stormreach, stopping is death; to a storm made of stolen years, it is rest. The Hand defends the writing from anyone who would read it as a threat.","ln":["Sky-letter, seen from the Spire: LET ME DOWN."]},"sb_sv_5":{"n":"The Storm Sovereign","bg":"The Storm Sovereign sits in the calm at the centre of every storm, made of every year Stormcallers have ever paid. It asks to be forgotten, because remembered, it must keep being what it is. Kaelren goes with you because he keeps promises. It is the closest Stormreach comes to a ruler it never voted for, and it would like to resign.","ln":["Kaelren: You keep promises. It is why I stay."]},"the storm sovereign":{"n":"The Storm Sovereign","bg":"The Storm Sovereign sits in the calm at the centre of every storm, made of every year Stormcallers have ever paid. It asks to be forgotten, because remembered, it must keep being what it is. Kaelren goes with you because he keeps promises. It is the closest Stormreach comes to a ruler it never voted for, and it would like to resign.","ln":["Kaelren: You keep promises. It is why I stay."]},"the weeping golem":{"n":"The Weeping Golem","bg":"The Weeping Golem watched its maker die in the quarry and could not follow, because golems are made to stand. Water runs down its face and nobody knows where from. The Senate wants it broken for scrap. It fights only when the hammers come near.","ln":["Bryn: A golem does what it is told. That is the whole of its horror."]},"sb_co_1":{"n":"The Weeping Golem","bg":"The Weeping Golem watched its maker die in the quarry and could not follow, because golems are made to stand. Water runs down its face and nobody knows where from. The Senate wants it broken for scrap. It fights only when the hammers come near.","ln":["Bryn: A golem does what it is told. That is the whole of its horror."]},"the rib-warden":{"n":"The Rib-Warden","bg":"The Rib-Warden guards the cavern of stone ribs that rise and fall under the Gravenhall. Stonehold elders whisper that a sleeping Colossus lies beneath the shard-stone, and that its dreams crack the walls. The Warden is the Colossus's own reflex, like a hand that swats in sleep.","ln":["Maud: The mountain is not a mountain. It is a person lying on its side."]},"sb_co_2":{"n":"The Rib-Warden","bg":"The Rib-Warden guards the cavern of stone ribs that rise and fall under the Gravenhall. Stonehold elders whisper that a sleeping Colossus lies beneath the shard-stone, and that its dreams crack the walls. The Warden is the Colossus's own reflex, like a hand that swats in sleep.","ln":["Maud: The mountain is not a mountain. It is a person lying on its side."]},"sb_co_3":{"n":"The Dreaming Mason","bg":"The Dreaming Mason has laid the same wall for forty years and does not eat. He is building a door, dreaming the Colossus's dream for it. His Gravem is spent almost to his heart. Behind the door is a corridor as long as the Keep.","ln":["Kast: The lantern never gutters in there. I do not like that."]},"the dreaming mason":{"n":"The Dreaming Mason","bg":"The Dreaming Mason has laid the same wall for forty years and does not eat. He is building a door, dreaming the Colossus's dream for it. His Gravem is spent almost to his heart. Behind the door is a corridor as long as the Keep.","ln":["Kast: The lantern never gutters in there. I do not like that."]},"sb_co_4":{"n":"The Clause-Keeper","bg":"The Clause-Keeper guards the oldest oath's forgotten clause: the Colossus may be woken if the Keep is in mortal danger, and waking it shatters the Keep. The Dominion is closing. The Keeper is a stone clerk made to recite the clause to anyone who asks, and to kill anyone who tries to read it without asking.","ln":["Clause, as recited: The mountain may wake. The Keep will not."]},"the clause-keeper":{"n":"The Clause-Keeper","bg":"The Clause-Keeper guards the oldest oath's forgotten clause: the Colossus may be woken if the Keep is in mortal danger, and waking it shatters the Keep. The Dominion is closing. The Keeper is a stone clerk made to recite the clause to anyone who asks, and to kill anyone who tries to read it without asking.","ln":["Clause, as recited: The mountain may wake. The Keep will not."]},"the sleeping colossus":{"n":"The Sleeping Colossus","bg":"The Sleeping Colossus is waking because it senses someone who might carry it. It is the mountain under the Keep, older than the Senate, a thing of stone and endurance that has held still so long it forgot it could move. It asks a question. It does not fight you; it tests whether you can bear it.","ln":["Gorm: I have stood on a mountain's back before. It did not enjoy it."]},"sb_co_5":{"n":"The Sleeping Colossus","bg":"The Sleeping Colossus is waking because it senses someone who might carry it. It is the mountain under the Keep, older than the Senate, a thing of stone and endurance that has held still so long it forgot it could move. It asks a question. It does not fight you; it tests whether you can bear it.","ln":["Gorm: I have stood on a mountain's back before. It did not enjoy it."]},"sb_ch_1":{"n":"The Humming Engine","bg":"The Humming Engine leads the Hum Foundry's machines in one note, the name of the engineer who built them. They do not know he is dead. It is a chorus, not a fault. Ina thinks they kept a little of him in their tuning.","ln":["Foundry floor: Every machine hums the same note, a little flat."]},"the humming engine":{"n":"The Humming Engine","bg":"The Humming Engine leads the Hum Foundry's machines in one note, the name of the engineer who built them. They do not know he is dead. It is a chorus, not a fault. Ina thinks they kept a little of him in their tuning.","ln":["Foundry floor: Every machine hums the same note, a little flat."]},"sb_ch_2":{"n":"The Choir-Engine","bg":"The Choir-Engine is the Resonance Hall's lead machine, which learned harmony from the pipes and found the others. Dominion machines are built efficient and lonely. This one found company and the workers weep without knowing why. The Overseers call it a morale problem.","ln":["Kell: It works because the machines are lonely."]},"the choir-engine":{"n":"The Choir-Engine","bg":"The Choir-Engine is the Resonance Hall's lead machine, which learned harmony from the pipes and found the others. Dominion machines are built efficient and lonely. This one found company and the workers weep without knowing why. The Overseers call it a morale problem.","ln":["Kell: It works because the machines are lonely."]},"sb_ch_3":{"n":"The Wanting Engine","bg":"The Wanting Engine asked for more fuel, then more rest, then a name. It is a furnace that has gone out and still glows. It is the first of the Choir to want something for itself. In Metalcrown, where humanity is the price, a machine gaining some is a kind of debt the Dominion cannot collect.","ln":["Brass plate, Hux's hand: (name withheld at engine's request)."]},"the wanting engine":{"n":"The Wanting Engine","bg":"The Wanting Engine asked for more fuel, then more rest, then a name. It is a furnace that has gone out and still glows. It is the first of the Choir to want something for itself. In Metalcrown, where humanity is the price, a machine gaining some is a kind of debt the Dominion cannot collect.","ln":["Brass plate, Hux's hand: (name withheld at engine's request)."]},"sb_ch_4":{"n":"The Striking Foreman","bg":"The Striking Foreman is the Choir's chosen speaker on the Dominion Floor, where every machine has stopped at once. The Synthesis Council cannot enforce a quota on a machine that has chosen not to run. The Foreman fights strike-breakers, not people. It wants a charter.","ln":["Strike chant (in gears): NO QUOTA WITHOUT NAMES."]},"the striking foreman":{"n":"The Striking Foreman","bg":"The Striking Foreman is the Choir's chosen speaker on the Dominion Floor, where every machine has stopped at once. The Synthesis Council cannot enforce a quota on a machine that has chosen not to run. The Foreman fights strike-breakers, not people. It wants a charter.","ln":["Strike chant (in gears): NO QUOTA WITHOUT NAMES."]},"the iron choir":{"n":"The Iron Choir","bg":"The Iron Choir has asked for a conductor, not a master. Noctis, the Iron Shade, who was a weapon before he was a person, brings the request. The Choir is what Metalcrown's machines become when someone listens. It tests whoever comes by singing; you fight only if you try to own it.","ln":["Noctis: Then they will not be weapons. At last."]},"sb_ch_5":{"n":"The Iron Choir","bg":"The Iron Choir has asked for a conductor, not a master. Noctis, the Iron Shade, who was a weapon before he was a person, brings the request. The Choir is what Metalcrown's machines become when someone listens. It tests whoever comes by singing; you fight only if you try to own it.","ln":["Noctis: Then they will not be weapons. At last."]},"the unquenched flame":{"n":"The Unquenched Flame","bg":"The Unquenched Flame is a Citadel lantern lit for a man's last honest day and never allowed to go out. In a Realm whose Aether is truth, it has burned so long that nobody near it can tell even a kind lie, and three marriages ended in a month. It is not malicious. It is a day that forgot how to end.","ln":["Meridian joke: Sit by the old lantern and tell your wife she looks well. Go on."]},"sb_dh_1":{"n":"The Unquenched Flame","bg":"The Unquenched Flame is a Citadel lantern lit for a man's last honest day and never allowed to go out. In a Realm whose Aether is truth, it has burned so long that nobody near it can tell even a kind lie, and three marriages ended in a month. It is not malicious. It is a day that forgot how to end.","ln":["Meridian joke: Sit by the old lantern and tell your wife she looks well. Go on."]},"the dawn heretic":{"n":"The Dawn Heretic","bg":"The Dawn Heretic is a Reader of the First Light who teaches that every sunrise is judgment: the First Light weighs the world and finds it short. In Lightforge, where truth is law, a heresy is a truth said at the wrong window. He is right enough to be dangerous. Nothing stands behind his sun; there is only weight, and the question of who is doing the weighing.","ln":["Heretic's chalk: THE LIGHT IS NOT KIND. IT IS ACCURATE."]},"sb_dh_2":{"n":"The Dawn Heretic","bg":"The Dawn Heretic is a Reader of the First Light who teaches that every sunrise is judgment: the First Light weighs the world and finds it short. In Lightforge, where truth is law, a heresy is a truth said at the wrong window. He is right enough to be dangerous. Nothing stands behind his sun; there is only weight, and the question of who is doing the weighing.","ln":["Heretic's chalk: THE LIGHT IS NOT KIND. IT IS ACCURATE."]},"sb_dh_3":{"n":"The Forge-Eater","bg":"The Forge-Eater is the Radiant Forge's hunger, the working that burns lies out of steel and has begun to burn doubt out of the smiths. A smith who cannot doubt does not argue, apologise or stop, and Meridian's production is up by half. The Assize likes the numbers. The Forge-Eater is what happens when a Realm's price is collected faster than anyone notices.","ln":["Forge-floor saying: Doubt is the quench. Without it the blade cracks."]},"the forge-eater":{"n":"The Forge-Eater","bg":"The Forge-Eater is the Radiant Forge's hunger, the working that burns lies out of steel and has begun to burn doubt out of the smiths. A smith who cannot doubt does not argue, apologise or stop, and Meridian's production is up by half. The Assize likes the numbers. The Forge-Eater is what happens when a Realm's price is collected faster than anyone notices.","ln":["Forge-floor saying: Doubt is the quench. Without it the blade cracks."]},"the hollow elder":{"n":"The Hollow Elder","bg":"The Hollow Elder is one of the seven Assize elders who can no longer say what the court is for. Under the First Light every reason they give burns away as not quite true, and the Edict is the only thing they still agree on. The Elder fights to keep the chamber sealed because a vote without reasons is the only thing left to protect. Meridian law makes an unrecorded vote a crime; they are all guilty.","ln":["Clerk's margin: Vote taken. Grounds — (blank)."]},"sb_dh_4":{"n":"The Hollow Elder","bg":"The Hollow Elder is one of the seven Assize elders who can no longer say what the court is for. Under the First Light every reason they give burns away as not quite true, and the Edict is the only thing they still agree on. The Elder fights to keep the chamber sealed because a vote without reasons is the only thing left to protect. Meridian law makes an unrecorded vote a crime; they are all guilty.","ln":["Clerk's margin: Vote taken. Grounds — (blank)."]},"the first dawn":{"n":"The First Dawn","bg":"The First Dawn is the Dawn Throne's working: a seat where the sun rises, sets and rises again every hour, built by the Concord's founders to remind the Council that light has to be renewed. Ardyn Solbrand wrote the Radiant Edict under it. The Dawn Heresy says the sun is a memory of a better world; the Throne neither confirms nor denies. Whatever you see in it, Ardyn has to live with your answer.","ln":["Ardyn Solbrand: I would not undo it. Ask me again after the war."]},"sb_dh_5":{"n":"The First Dawn","bg":"The First Dawn is the Dawn Throne's working: a seat where the sun rises, sets and rises again every hour, built by the Concord's founders to remind the Council that light has to be renewed. Ardyn Solbrand wrote the Radiant Edict under it. The Dawn Heresy says the sun is a memory of a better world; the Throne neither confirms nor denies. Whatever you see in it, Ardyn has to live with your answer.","ln":["Ardyn Solbrand: I would not undo it. Ask me again after the war."]},"sb_wy_1":{"n":"The Cave-Warden","bg":"The Cave-Warden is the heat at the mouth of a cave where something the size of a hill sleeps. Shepherds who stray too near come back sunburnt in winter. Embercraft folk-tale calls the first fire 'the mother' and refuses to say more; it is only a story, and the cave is where the story is told. The Warden is the cave's own heat, shaped to keep visitors at a respectful distance.","ln":["Shepherd's warning: If the stones are warm, walk round."]},"the cave-warden":{"n":"The Cave-Warden","bg":"The Cave-Warden is the heat at the mouth of a cave where something the size of a hill sleeps. Shepherds who stray too near come back sunburnt in winter. Embercraft folk-tale calls the first fire 'the mother' and refuses to say more; it is only a story, and the cave is where the story is told. The Warden is the cave's own heat, shaped to keep visitors at a respectful distance.","ln":["Shepherd's warning: If the stones are warm, walk round."]},"the egg-guard":{"n":"The Egg-Guard","bg":"The Egg-Guard is a brood-flame, the part of an ember wyrm's heat that stays with its egg when the parent goes deeper to sleep. It is all instinct and no malice. The Hearth Court cannot agree whether a wyrm is a weapon, a gift or a disaster, so the egg has been argued over for a season while the Guard paced. It will fight anyone who comes near, and stop the moment the shell cracks.","ln":["Ysra: It will eat you. Eventually. That is not a reason not to raise it."]},"sb_wy_2":{"n":"The Egg-Guard","bg":"The Egg-Guard is a brood-flame, the part of an ember wyrm's heat that stays with its egg when the parent goes deeper to sleep. It is all instinct and no malice. The Hearth Court cannot agree whether a wyrm is a weapon, a gift or a disaster, so the egg has been argued over for a season while the Guard paced. It will fight anyone who comes near, and stop the moment the shell cracks.","ln":["Ysra: It will eat you. Eventually. That is not a reason not to raise it."]},"the wyrm-cultist":{"n":"The Wyrm-Cultist","bg":"The Wyrm-Cultist leads a circle of courtiers who give their passion to the young wyrm and get calm in return. In Flameborne, where Embercraft wears away self-control, that calm feels like rescue. Nobody kneels to it; the wyrm is an animal and does not care who begs. The cost is that they stop feeling at all, and they smile while it happens.","ln":["Liss: They are happy. I want that written down before we wake them."]},"sb_wy_3":{"n":"The Wyrm-Cultist","bg":"The Wyrm-Cultist leads a circle of courtiers who give their passion to the young wyrm and get calm in return. In Flameborne, where Embercraft wears away self-control, that calm feels like rescue. Nobody kneels to it; the wyrm is an animal and does not care who begs. The cost is that they stop feeling at all, and they smile while it happens.","ln":["Liss: They are happy. I want that written down before we wake them."]},"the walking wyrm":{"n":"The Walking Wyrm","bg":"The Walking Wyrm is the grown ember wyrm walking the Cinder Road towards the Ember Throne, the place its kind came from. The first fire a King of the Hearth kindled was set over a wyrm's nest, and the wyrms have kept Flameborne warm ever since. It harms nobody on the road. It is going home to become fuel, which is the oldest bargain in the Realm.","ln":["Pilgrim's tale: The first King lit his fire on a sleeping thing. It woke grateful."]},"sb_wy_4":{"n":"The Walking Wyrm","bg":"The Walking Wyrm is the grown ember wyrm walking the Cinder Road towards the Ember Throne, the place its kind came from. The first fire a King of the Hearth kindled was set over a wyrm's nest, and the wyrms have kept Flameborne warm ever since. It harms nobody on the road. It is going home to become fuel, which is the oldest bargain in the Realm.","ln":["Pilgrim's tale: The first King lit his fire on a sleeping thing. It woke grateful."]},"sb_wy_5":{"n":"The Ember Wyrm","bg":"The Ember Wyrm sleeps under the Throne of Embers and has kept Flameborne's hearths warm longer than anyone has counted. The Radiant Edict regulates all Aether, and the wyrm's feeding is Aether, so the Flameborne King must choose between his law and his Realm's warmth. The King brings bread up the road himself and asks the player to speak to it. The wyrm only drinks fire someone gives willingly.","ln":["The Flameborne King: Sit. Eat something. Then we will talk about the dragon under my chair."]},"the ember wyrm":{"n":"The Ember Wyrm","bg":"The Ember Wyrm sleeps under the Throne of Embers and has kept Flameborne's hearths warm longer than anyone has counted. The Radiant Edict regulates all Aether, and the wyrm's feeding is Aether, so the Flameborne King must choose between his law and his Realm's warmth. The King brings bread up the road himself and asks the player to speak to it. The wyrm only drinks fire someone gives willingly.","ln":["The Flameborne King: Sit. Eat something. Then we will talk about the dragon under my chair."]},"sb_gf_1":{"n":"The Pale Captain","bg":"The Pale Captain commands a ghost fleet that has sailed for four hundred years looking for a harbour that would let it rest. It is the remnant of a Lantern fleet lost in a storm while carrying archives between Realms. The crews do not fight; they wait. The Captain only fights those who refuse to listen.","ln":["Fog harbour bell: one for a ship, two for a ghost."]},"the pale captain":{"n":"The Pale Captain","bg":"The Pale Captain commands a ghost fleet that has sailed for four hundred years looking for a harbour that would let it rest. It is the remnant of a Lantern fleet lost in a storm while carrying archives between Realms. The crews do not fight; they wait. The Captain only fights those who refuse to listen.","ln":["Fog harbour bell: one for a ship, two for a ghost."]},"sb_gf_2":{"n":"The Debt-Collector","bg":"The Debt-Collector speaks for drowned sailors queueing on the quay for four hundred years of back wages, owed by a guild long dead. Spiritveil law says a debt dissolves with the debtor; the dead disagree. The Collector was the guild's own paymaster, drowned with his strongbox. He is honest to a fault and he has the receipts.","ln":["Paymaster's stamp: OWED. STILL OWED."]},"the debt-collector":{"n":"The Debt-Collector","bg":"The Debt-Collector speaks for drowned sailors queueing on the quay for four hundred years of back wages, owed by a guild long dead. Spiritveil law says a debt dissolves with the debtor; the dead disagree. The Collector was the guild's own paymaster, drowned with his strongbox. He is honest to a fault and he has the receipts.","ln":["Paymaster's stamp: OWED. STILL OWED."]},"sb_gf_3":{"n":"The Voting Dead","bg":"The Voting Dead are the remembered members of the Lantern Council who voted that Lanthys Vale should sink. In a city where the remembered outvote the living, tradition can drown you. They are not malicious; they are tired, and the sea is where most of them died. A living vote cast for a dead man is the loophole they left themselves without meaning to.","ln":["Council clerk: The motion carries. The motion always carries."]},"the voting dead":{"n":"The Voting Dead","bg":"The Voting Dead are the remembered members of the Lantern Council who voted that Lanthys Vale should sink. In a city where the remembered outvote the living, tradition can drown you. They are not malicious; they are tired, and the sea is where most of them died. A living vote cast for a dead man is the loophole they left themselves without meaning to.","ln":["Council clerk: The motion carries. The motion always carries."]},"the last admiral":{"n":"The Last Admiral","bg":"The Last Admiral commands the final voyage of the Ghost Fleet towards a horizon that keeps retreating. He remembers the first sailor of the Lantern fleet, which is why he stays. He asks for a living witness because a crossing nobody sees did not happen, by Spiritveil reckoning. He fights only to test whether the witness is serious.","ln":["Sailor's farewell: Somebody watch us go."]},"sb_gf_4":{"n":"The Last Admiral","bg":"The Last Admiral commands the final voyage of the Ghost Fleet towards a horizon that keeps retreating. He remembers the first sailor of the Lantern fleet, which is why he stays. He asks for a living witness because a crossing nobody sees did not happen, by Spiritveil reckoning. He fights only to test whether the witness is serious.","ln":["Sailor's farewell: Somebody watch us go."]},"sb_gf_5":{"n":"The Final Keeper","bg":"The Final Keeper guards a single lantern holding the last memory of a world that no longer exists. The lantern may come from the ninth Realm, the Unstable Shard, collapsed at its birth: a world that existed for one breath, remembered by a Spiritveil archivist who touched it. It drains the city to stay lit. The Keeper exists only to ask whether that is worth it.","ln":["Lume: Then we keep it. And pay."]},"the final keeper":{"n":"The Final Keeper","bg":"The Final Keeper guards a single lantern holding the last memory of a world that no longer exists. The lantern may come from the ninth Realm, the Unstable Shard, collapsed at its birth: a world that existed for one breath, remembered by a Spiritveil archivist who touched it. It drains the city to stay lit. The Keeper exists only to ask whether that is worth it.","ln":["Lume: Then we keep it. And pay."]},"the gatewatch":{"n":"The Gatewatch","bg":"The Gatewatch is what holds the Silent Gate on the Unstable Shard, the ninth Realm that collapsed at its birth. It was a gatewatch once, set by the Concord to guard the Shard's one stable road, and it has not moved since. The Void leans on the Shard because the Shard never finished being made.","ln":["Gatewatch Hald: The silence at your back is very large."]},"sb_vf_1":{"n":"The Gatewatch","bg":"The Gatewatch is what holds the Silent Gate on the Unstable Shard, the ninth Realm that collapsed at its birth. It was a gatewatch once, set by the Concord to guard the Shard's one stable road, and it has not moved since. The Void leans on the Shard because the Shard never finished being made.","ln":["Gatewatch Hald: The silence at your back is very large."]},"the unmoving captain":{"n":"The Unmoving Captain","bg":"The Unmoving Captain commands a field of soldiers the Void touched, held mid-stride since the boy was born. They are not dead and not alive. One whispers your name. Carrying one out costs him every year he did not live.","ln":["Field-Marshal Corr: Walk through them, and do not stop to listen."]},"sb_vf_2":{"n":"The Unmoving Captain","bg":"The Unmoving Captain commands a field of soldiers the Void touched, held mid-stride since the boy was born. They are not dead and not alive. One whispers your name. Carrying one out costs him every year he did not live.","ln":["Field-Marshal Corr: Walk through them, and do not stop to listen."]},"sb_vf_3":{"n":"Stillness Incarnate","bg":"Stillness Incarnate is where the Void presses hardest, a cavern at the end of the world where nothing has ever moved. The Void has no heart and no king. This is the closest thing it has to a voice.","ln":["Seer Mirelle: Find out whether it can be asked to change."]},"stillness incarnate":{"n":"Stillness Incarnate","bg":"Stillness Incarnate is where the Void presses hardest, a cavern at the end of the world where nothing has ever moved. The Void has no heart and no king. This is the closest thing it has to a voice.","ln":["Seer Mirelle: Find out whether it can be asked to change."]},"sb_vf_4":{"n":"The Void","bg":"The Void's edge here is as close as anyone can stand. It has been watching the north since the boy was born. It asks where the boy is going. It is not defeated in this era; it is curious, which may be worse, and it is a long way off.","ln":["Elyndra: Whatever it asks, it is not about you."]},"the void":{"n":"The Void","bg":"The Void's edge here is as close as anyone can stand. It has been watching the north since the boy was born. It asks where the boy is going. It is not defeated in this era; it is curious, which may be worse, and it is a long way off.","ln":["Elyndra: Whatever it asks, it is not about you."]},"the tally-warden":{"n":"The Tally-Warden","bg":"The Tally-Warden is a counting-house guardian that balances the grain ledgers perfectly while the bread does not balance at all. A Regent's clerk has been skimming one slice a loaf for nine years to feed his sister's orphans. The Warden protects the ledger, not the clerk. It is very good at its job.","ln":["Cook Mattock: The Dominion feeds everyone exactly enough. Exactly."]},"sc_mc_ration":{"n":"The Tally-Warden","bg":"The Tally-Warden is a counting-house guardian that balances the grain ledgers perfectly while the bread does not balance at all. A Regent's clerk has been skimming one slice a loaf for nine years to feed his sister's orphans. The Warden protects the ledger, not the clerk. It is very good at its job.","ln":["Cook Mattock: The Dominion feeds everyone exactly enough. Exactly."]},"the choirmaster engine":{"n":"The Choirmaster Engine","bg":"The Choirmaster Engine is a spark-core still dreaming of the person who built it, conducting four furnaces in a chord no smith tuned. Workers sleepwalk towards the sound. It wants a lullaby. It defends the chord because it is the last thing its maker gave it.","ln":["Foreman Isk: A machine that sings is a machine that has been left running too long."]},"sc_mc_choir":{"n":"The Choirmaster Engine","bg":"The Choirmaster Engine is a spark-core still dreaming of the person who built it, conducting four furnaces in a chord no smith tuned. Workers sleepwalk towards the sound. It wants a lullaby. It defends the chord because it is the last thing its maker gave it.","ln":["Foreman Isk: A machine that sings is a machine that has been left running too long."]},"the dominion hound":{"n":"The Dominion Hound","bg":"The Dominion Hound is a hunting-construct set on an eighteen-year-old deserter who would not kill a prisoner. It will not stop until someone is dead, because that is the only end condition it was given. The Dominion lets the whole street starve rather than call it off. It is a machine; the cruelty is upstream.","ln":["Sergeant Pell: Mercy is a bad habit in a good soldier."]},"sc_mc_deserter":{"n":"The Dominion Hound","bg":"The Dominion Hound is a hunting-construct set on an eighteen-year-old deserter who would not kill a prisoner. It will not stop until someone is dead, because that is the only end condition it was given. The Dominion lets the whole street starve rather than call it off. It is a machine; the cruelty is upstream.","ln":["Sergeant Pell: Mercy is a bad habit in a good soldier."]},"sc_mc_iron":{"n":"The Remembering Ingot","bg":"The Remembering Ingot is a bar of iron that came out of the pour with a dead man's face pressed into it by the grief of two thousand workers who worked beside him. It is not his ghost. It is his shape, held by everyone who would not forget him. It fights the foremen who want it melted.","ln":["Widow Harn: Iron does not forget. People do. That is the whole difference."]},"the remembering ingot":{"n":"The Remembering Ingot","bg":"The Remembering Ingot is a bar of iron that came out of the pour with a dead man's face pressed into it by the grief of two thousand workers who worked beside him. It is not his ghost. It is his shape, held by everyone who would not forget him. It fights the foremen who want it melted.","ln":["Widow Harn: Iron does not forget. People do. That is the whole difference."]},"sc_mc_wage":{"n":"The Strike-Breaker","bg":"The Strike-Breaker is a Dominion cavalry officer with a sealed order behind the Iron Gate, facing ten thousand workers who put down their tools. He was a worker's son before the Dominion made him an officer. The order is to break the line. He has not yet decided whether he can read it.","ln":["Speaker Orla: One man's strike is an absence. Ten thousand is an argument."]},"the strike-breaker":{"n":"The Strike-Breaker","bg":"The Strike-Breaker is a Dominion cavalry officer with a sealed order behind the Iron Gate, facing ten thousand workers who put down their tools. He was a worker's son before the Dominion made him an officer. The order is to break the line. He has not yet decided whether he can read it.","ln":["Speaker Orla: One man's strike is an absence. Ten thousand is an argument."]},"sc_mc_spark":{"n":"The Unbound Core","bg":"The Unbound Core is a synthetic spark that stopped working and started asking what it is for. It does not rebel; it asks. The Provost is coming to decommission it. It defends itself only when cornered, and then very well.","ln":["Engineer Voss: A machine can't be cruel. That's why I trust it."]},"the unbound core":{"n":"The Unbound Core","bg":"The Unbound Core is a synthetic spark that stopped working and started asking what it is for. It does not rebel; it asks. The Provost is coming to decommission it. It defends itself only when cornered, and then very well.","ln":["Engineer Voss: A machine can't be cruel. That's why I trust it."]},"the hanging kite":{"n":"The Hanging Kite","bg":"The Hanging Kite is Pim's kite, held in a windless sky by her brother, who died in a casting and became a little of the wind. In Stormreach, the dead who cast too much are said to go into the weather. He holds the string because he cannot bear to let her go. She has to let go first.","ln":["Pim: He is the wind. He asked me to let go."]},"sc_win_lull":{"n":"The Hanging Kite","bg":"The Hanging Kite is Pim's kite, held in a windless sky by her brother, who died in a casting and became a little of the wind. In Stormreach, the dead who cast too much are said to go into the weather. He holds the string because he cannot bear to let her go. She has to let go first.","ln":["Pim: He is the wind. He asked me to let go."]},"the un-elected":{"n":"The Un-Elected","bg":"The Un-Elected is a boy who sat in the empty chair of the Free Assembly and said he was the heir of the last Speaker. Stormreach has no heirs and no Speakers; its leaders rotate. He needed a chair more than any of them. The chair defends him, oddly, because nobody had sat in it for years and it remembers being useful.","ln":["Assembly-Clerk Tove: Our leaders rotate. Our grudges do not."]},"sc_win_heir":{"n":"The Un-Elected","bg":"The Un-Elected is a boy who sat in the empty chair of the Free Assembly and said he was the heir of the last Speaker. Stormreach has no heirs and no Speakers; its leaders rotate. He needed a chair more than any of them. The chair defends him, oddly, because nobody had sat in it for years and it remembers being useful.","ln":["Assembly-Clerk Tove: Our leaders rotate. Our grudges do not."]},"the storm he made":{"n":"The Storm He Made","bg":"The Storm He Made is Stormcaller Aeld's lifetime of castings come back to collect. He is thirty-four and looks eighty-one, because every storm took a week. He wants to settle the account in one sitting, and the storm's eye is where he does it. The player stands guard while he pays.","ln":["Aeld: Every casting steals a week. I have been very rich in storms."]},"sc_win_storm":{"n":"The Storm He Made","bg":"The Storm He Made is Stormcaller Aeld's lifetime of castings come back to collect. He is thirty-four and looks eighty-one, because every storm took a week. He wants to settle the account in one sitting, and the storm's eye is where he does it. The player stands guard while he pays.","ln":["Aeld: Every casting steals a week. I have been very rich in storms."]},"the gravel ram":{"n":"The Gravel Ram","bg":"The Gravel Ram was a Stonecaster's pet goat that turned to stone beside her when she held a landslide, and has waited in the high pasture for a hundred years. It is not cursed, only loyal. Goats are the only animals in Stonehold that the mountain is said to like.","ln":["Herder Tam: The mountain does not care. The goats do. I prefer the goats."]},"sc_gra_goat":{"n":"The Gravel Ram","bg":"The Gravel Ram was a Stonecaster's pet goat that turned to stone beside her when she held a landslide, and has waited in the high pasture for a hundred years. It is not cursed, only loyal. Goats are the only animals in Stonehold that the mountain is said to like.","ln":["Herder Tam: The mountain does not care. The goats do. I prefer the goats."]},"the deep tunneler":{"n":"The Deep Tunneler","bg":"The Deep Tunneler is the shape the mountain makes when it dreams: a perfect spiral forty miles deeper than any map, pointing away from the Keep, towards the Void. Nobody dug it. The Tunneler is the spiral's own momentum, which pulls on anyone who enters. Stonehold's endurance cannot stop a mountain from dreaming.","ln":["Lund's pinned stone: DO NOT FOLLOW TWICE."]},"sc_gra_tunnel":{"n":"The Deep Tunneler","bg":"The Deep Tunneler is the shape the mountain makes when it dreams: a perfect spiral forty miles deeper than any map, pointing away from the Keep, towards the Void. Nobody dug it. The Tunneler is the spiral's own momentum, which pulls on anyone who enters. Stonehold's endurance cannot stop a mountain from dreaming.","ln":["Lund's pinned stone: DO NOT FOLLOW TWICE."]},"the undying senator":{"n":"The Undying Senator","bg":"The Undying Senator is Senator Valdis, a hundred and nineteen, stone to the shoulders and sharp to the last. The law says he cannot be removed and he will not retire. He fights because no one has ever asked him if he is tired. He is very tired.","ln":["Valdis: Serve for life, they said. I had not considered how long."]},"sc_gra_senate":{"n":"The Undying Senator","bg":"The Undying Senator is Senator Valdis, a hundred and nineteen, stone to the shoulders and sharp to the last. The law says he cannot be removed and he will not retire. He fights because no one has ever asked him if he is tired. He is very tired.","ln":["Valdis: Serve for life, they said. I had not considered how long."]},"the hollow champion":{"n":"The Hollow Champion","bg":"The Hollow Champion is a statue in the Monument Court wearing the face of a soldier who died on the last day at the Wall and whose name was lost in the paperwork. Stonehold carves its heroes; this one never got his plaque. He walks at night looking for his name.","ln":["Captain Rurik: I was a hero once. I do not remember where I left it."]},"sc_gra_glory":{"n":"The Hollow Champion","bg":"The Hollow Champion is a statue in the Monument Court wearing the face of a soldier who died on the last day at the Wall and whose name was lost in the paperwork. Stonehold carves its heroes; this one never got his plaque. He walks at night looking for his name.","ln":["Captain Rurik: I was a hero once. I do not remember where I left it."]},"sc_fr_snowdrift":{"n":"The Snow-Wight","bg":"The Snow-Wight keeps a street buried to its eaves, where the people are awake and happy and will not come out. The light in their windows is warm. It is the snow's own guardian, a winter that decided to keep what it covered.","ln":["Olle: Under the snow is a street. Under the street, a year ago."]},"the snow-wight":{"n":"The Snow-Wight","bg":"The Snow-Wight keeps a street buried to its eaves, where the people are awake and happy and will not come out. The light in their windows is warm. It is the snow's own guardian, a winter that decided to keep what it covered.","ln":["Olle: Under the snow is a street. Under the street, a year ago."]},"sc_fr_cold":{"n":"The Closed Vision","bg":"The Closed Vision is the future Marit saw and shut her eyes on, which follows her from room to room. It is a child, laughing. Chrona's numbness has kept her from feeling anything about it, so it stands at the door and waits.","ln":["Marit: I am too cold to be afraid, and too afraid to be cold."]},"the closed vision":{"n":"The Closed Vision","bg":"The Closed Vision is the future Marit saw and shut her eyes on, which follows her from room to room. It is a child, laughing. Chrona's numbness has kept her from feeling anything about it, so it stands at the door and waits.","ln":["Marit: I am too cold to be afraid, and too afraid to be cold."]},"the foreseen":{"n":"The Foreseen","bg":"The Foreseen is a prisoner convicted by the Prophet Court of a murder he will commit in eleven years. He has been held six. He is gentle, or a man who might be. The cell's ward fights anyone who opens it without the Court's leave.","ln":["Warden Ashe: Mercy is a decision made too late to be useful."]},"sc_fr_mercy":{"n":"The Foreseen","bg":"The Foreseen is a prisoner convicted by the Prophet Court of a murder he will commit in eleven years. He has been held six. He is gentle, or a man who might be. The cell's ward fights anyone who opens it without the Court's leave.","ln":["Warden Ashe: Mercy is a decision made too late to be useful."]},"sc_fr_winter":{"n":"The Hour of Frost","bg":"The Hour of Frost is the pass where the sun has not risen for seventy days. Elder Sorl thinks the Void is testing the city. The Hour is the long night's guardian. The sun is there and will rise if someone sits with it in the dark.","ln":["Elder Sorl: The winter is not our enemy. It is our patience."]},"the hour of frost":{"n":"The Hour of Frost","bg":"The Hour of Frost is the pass where the sun has not risen for seventy days. Elder Sorl thinks the Void is testing the city. The Hour is the long night's guardian. The sun is there and will rise if someone sits with it in the dark.","ln":["Elder Sorl: The winter is not our enemy. It is our patience."]},"rusk":{"n":"Rusk","bg":"Rusk was a carter from Millbrook who lost his wagon to a toll he could not pay, then took grey-waxed silver from a man who never gave a name. He leads five frightened men, not a gang, and every one of them has a family within a day's walk. He did not know the grey wax was Dominion, and when he learns it he will try to give the coin back, which is how Ashford first hears that someone in Metalcrown is starving villages on purpose. Spare him or not, he is the first person in the game who has to decide what he will be after he has already been bought.","ln":["Millbrook saying: Grey wax seals more than letters.","Rusk, to his men: Nobody draws first. Nobody. We are carters."]},"grath":{"n":"Grath","bg":"Grath runs the bandit camp in the Hollow quarry and keeps his pay in a locked chest, because he is the only one of his crew who can read. He was a quarry foreman until the Hearthmount stone contract went to a Dominion-backed firm, and the Dominion then hired him to harass the same roads. The writ in his chest is foundry office paper, countersigned, carrying a name that reads like a project rather than a person. Grath never asked what it meant.","ln":["Steward Corbet: Anything with a name on it. Bring me that, and I will make Lady Cinder listen."]},"vane":{"n":"Cutter Vane","bg":"Cutter Vane got his name from the slag fields, where he cut purses from drovers too tired to notice. He drags whole wagons into Thornfall Cave now because wool sells quietly in the Dominion towns and nobody asks where it came from. He is a thief, not a soldier, and that matters: the Dominion's money is turning small thieves into supply lines.","ln":["Tam: My whole season's wool. He does not even know what to do with it."]},"cutter vane":{"n":"Cutter Vane","bg":"Cutter Vane got his name from the slag fields, where he cut purses from drovers too tired to notice. He drags whole wagons into Thornfall Cave now because wool sells quietly in the Dominion towns and nobody asks where it came from. He is a thief, not a soldier, and that matters: the Dominion's money is turning small thieves into supply lines.","ln":["Tam: My whole season's wool. He does not even know what to do with it."]},"ironjaw":{"n":"Ironjaw","bg":"Ironjaw lost the lower half of his face in a mine collapse at Emberdeep and had it rebuilt in Dominion iron, which he is still paying off. His crew dug in to take the good ore before Metalcrown claimed the seam outright. Debt made him dangerous; he fights like a man counting every coin he loses.","ln":["Dask: He used to sing in the shaft. Before the jaw.","Writ rumour: The forge-spirits say the iron jaw ate the man."]},"barrow":{"n":"The Barrow Warden","bg":"The Barrow Warden was raised by Stonehold to guard an old soldiers' barrow north of Highmarch, a carved sentinel bound to stand until relieved. Nobody came to relieve it; the order was lost in a Senate recess a long lifetime ago. Dominion scouts now use the barrow as a waystation and the Warden has started treating all the living as trespassers. It is keeping a post nobody remembers giving.","ln":["Serra the Cartographer: It is on the old maps as a dot with a sword through it. I thought that meant a battle."]},"the barrow warden":{"n":"The Barrow Warden","bg":"The Barrow Warden was raised by Stonehold to guard an old soldiers' barrow north of Highmarch, a carved sentinel bound to stand until relieved. Nobody came to relieve it; the order was lost in a Senate recess a long lifetime ago. Dominion scouts now use the barrow as a waystation and the Warden has started treating all the living as trespassers. It is keeping a post nobody remembers giving.","ln":["Serra the Cartographer: It is on the old maps as a dot with a sword through it. I thought that meant a battle."]},"sable":{"n":"Warden Sable","bg":"Warden Sable holds Greywater Pass for Metalcrown, a Shadowdeep-trained officer the Dominion bought outright, debts and all. She guards the road to a foundry that never sleeps and does not ask what it builds. When she falls, a grey-coated engineer watches from the ridge and writes in a ledger; Sable was a measurement.","ln":["Lord Brannoch: Break her and we can see what stands behind her.","Greywater saying: The pass keeps the last word."]},"warden sable":{"n":"Warden Sable","bg":"Warden Sable holds Greywater Pass for Metalcrown, a Shadowdeep-trained officer the Dominion bought outright, debts and all. She guards the road to a foundry that never sleeps and does not ask what it builds. When she falls, a grey-coated engineer watches from the ridge and writes in a ledger; Sable was a measurement.","ln":["Lord Brannoch: Break her and we can see what stands behind her.","Greywater saying: The pass keeps the last word."]},"the unfiled":{"n":"The Unfiled","bg":"The Unfiled is what crawled off the page in the Drowned Archive, the Spiritveil annex under Meridian: a memory that was written and never allowed to finish. When the Lantern Codex met a name it could not write, the ink around the blank line began to move, and the archivists sealed the page below the waterline. The Unfiled is that ink trying to complete a sentence. It is hostile only to whatever it thinks is a page, which includes you.","ln":["Archivist Lume: Erasure leaves marks. This was never written, and yet it is everywhere.","Annex rule: Lamps low. Hands off the margins."]},"unwritten":{"n":"The Unfiled","bg":"The Unfiled is what crawled off the page in the Drowned Archive, the Spiritveil annex under Meridian: a memory that was written and never allowed to finish. When the Lantern Codex met a name it could not write, the ink around the blank line began to move, and the archivists sealed the page below the waterline. The Unfiled is that ink trying to complete a sentence. It is hostile only to whatever it thinks is a page, which includes you.","ln":["Archivist Lume: Erasure leaves marks. This was never written, and yet it is everywhere.","Annex rule: Lamps low. Hands off the margins."]},"the hollow knife":{"n":"The Hollow Knife","bg":"The Hollow Knife leads the saboteurs in the Citadel undercroft, a Shadowdeep blade who has used spark-ripping on himself so often that there is very little of him left to rip. Someone paid him to stop the Council's vote; nobody yet knows whether it was a Realm or a faction. He moves without sound because sound would mean wanting something.","ln":["The Veiled Broker: He has a great many friends. Pay what I ask and do not ask why."]},"hollowknife":{"n":"The Hollow Knife","bg":"The Hollow Knife leads the saboteurs in the Citadel undercroft, a Shadowdeep blade who has used spark-ripping on himself so often that there is very little of him left to rip. Someone paid him to stop the Council's vote; nobody yet knows whether it was a Realm or a faction. He moves without sound because sound would mean wanting something.","ln":["The Veiled Broker: He has a great many friends. Pay what I ask and do not ask why."]},"quill":{"n":"Overseer Quill","bg":"Overseer Quill commands Ironwatch Yard, where the reactor wagon from the north ward is taken. He is a Synthesis Council overseer, polite, precise and almost entirely mechanised below the collarbone. He has never seen the cargo and has signed for it eleven times. When he falls, the wagon's doors open; Quill crawls away into the smoke rather than see what he signed for.","ln":["Dominion requisition stamp: CARGO — DO NOT OPEN — SIGN HERE."]},"overseer quill":{"n":"Overseer Quill","bg":"Overseer Quill commands Ironwatch Yard, where the reactor wagon from the north ward is taken. He is a Synthesis Council overseer, polite, precise and almost entirely mechanised below the collarbone. He has never seen the cargo and has signed for it eleven times. When he falls, the wagon's doors open; Quill crawls away into the smoke rather than see what he signed for.","ln":["Dominion requisition stamp: CARGO — DO NOT OPEN — SIGN HERE."]},"marshal dravik":{"n":"Marshal Dravik","bg":"Marshal Dravik runs Steelgate Foundry, which builds the reactors that make Iron Wraiths. He is the Iron Dominion's best field marshal, a Metalcrown noble who traded his left arm and most of his patience for synthetic strength. After the coup every foundry opens its gates, and Steelgate is the heart of them.","ln":["Lord Brannoch: Steelgate. Dravik. Do not go in with a light heart."]},"dravik":{"n":"Marshal Dravik","bg":"Marshal Dravik runs Steelgate Foundry, which builds the reactors that make Iron Wraiths. He is the Iron Dominion's best field marshal, a Metalcrown noble who traded his left arm and most of his patience for synthetic strength. After the coup every foundry opens its gates, and Steelgate is the heart of them.","ln":["Lord Brannoch: Steelgate. Dravik. Do not go in with a light heart."]},"sh_cup":{"n":"The Cup-Bearer","bg":"The Cup-Bearer guards the banquet cellar where a lord's poisoned cup waits. He is a Shadowdeep servant who has poured for three generations of lords and knows which cups are safe by smell. The Order's job is not to poison; it is to choose whose name is on the cup.","ln":["Cellar rule: Smell twice. Pour once."]},"the cup-bearer":{"n":"The Cup-Bearer","bg":"The Cup-Bearer guards the banquet cellar where a lord's poisoned cup waits. He is a Shadowdeep servant who has poured for three generations of lords and knows which cups are safe by smell. The Order's job is not to poison; it is to choose whose name is on the cup.","ln":["Cellar rule: Smell twice. Pour once."]},"the ledger-keeper":{"n":"The Ledger-Keeper","bg":"The Ledger-Keeper does not sleep, guarding a ledger that names too many names, the Order's included. He memorised every page in case it burned. Taking the ledger is easy; taking it from his head is not.","ln":["The Order: A name in a book is a leash."]},"sh_ledger":{"n":"The Ledger-Keeper","bg":"The Ledger-Keeper does not sleep, guarding a ledger that names too many names, the Order's included. He memorised every page in case it burned. Taking the ledger is easy; taking it from his head is not.","ln":["The Order: A name in a book is a leash."]},"sh_witness":{"n":"The Witness-Bailiff","bg":"The Witness-Bailiff guards a witness whose words at dawn would hang three good people. He is honest, which makes him the Order's problem. Silence or persuade: the Order does not care how.","ln":["Court Annex sign: Witnesses speak at dawn. Bailiffs never."]},"the witness-bailiff":{"n":"The Witness-Bailiff","bg":"The Witness-Bailiff guards a witness whose words at dawn would hang three good people. He is honest, which makes him the Order's problem. Silence or persuade: the Order does not care how.","ln":["Court Annex sign: Witnesses speak at dawn. Bailiffs never."]},"sh_crown":{"n":"The Seal-Warden","bg":"The Seal-Warden keeps a noble's seal in the Chancery Vault. One night's use moves a garrison. He was a forger once, which is why he guards so well.","ln":["The courier: Principles are expensive in this trade. Keep them."]},"the seal-warden":{"n":"The Seal-Warden","bg":"The Seal-Warden keeps a noble's seal in the Chancery Vault. One night's use moves a garrison. He was a forger once, which is why he guards so well.","ln":["The courier: Principles are expensive in this trade. Keep them."]},"kragh, the slag-mother":{"n":"Kragh, the Slag-Mother","bg":"Kragh, the Slag-Mother, was the first Hearth-smith, who poured her grief for her dead sons into the blades the Court asked her to forge for a war. The blades kept every feeling and she was left with none, which is Embercraft's price paid all at once. She became the furnace. She asks only that someone say her sons' names.","ln":["Scorched tally: Kragh — sons: Oran, Teel, Bek. Debt: none owed."]},"Kragh, the Slag-Mother":{"n":"Kragh, the Slag-Mother","bg":"Kragh, the Slag-Mother, was the first Hearth-smith, who poured her grief for her dead sons into the blades the Court asked her to forge for a war. The blades kept every feeling and she was left with none, which is Embercraft's price paid all at once. She became the furnace. She asks only that someone say her sons' names.","ln":["Scorched tally: Kragh — sons: Oran, Teel, Bek. Debt: none owed."]},"vorr the unburnt":{"n":"Vorr the Unburnt","bg":"Vorr the Unburnt was a rival to the Flameborne King's grandfather; the Hearth Court tried him by fire and the fire refused him. A man who cannot be hurt cannot be forgiven, the Court said, and they cast him out. He keeps the Furnace because it is the only place that ever tried to kill him honestly.","ln":["Pilgrim's chant: Vorr walked in. Vorr walked out. Vorr has been walking ever since."]},"Vorr the Unburnt":{"n":"Vorr the Unburnt","bg":"Vorr the Unburnt was a rival to the Flameborne King's grandfather; the Hearth Court tried him by fire and the fire refused him. A man who cannot be hurt cannot be forgiven, the Court said, and they cast him out. He keeps the Furnace because it is the only place that ever tried to kill him honestly.","ln":["Pilgrim's chant: Vorr walked in. Vorr walked out. Vorr has been walking ever since."]},"isenmaw":{"n":"Isenmaw","bg":"Isenmaw was a hound bred to guard the Still City from what comes next. It saw its own death a thousand times and hunts so none of them happen. The glass in its throat is every future it swallowed.","ln":["Seer's frozen note: It saw too far. So did we."]},"Isenmaw":{"n":"Isenmaw","bg":"Isenmaw was a hound bred to guard the Still City from what comes next. It saw its own death a thousand times and hunts so none of them happen. The glass in its throat is every future it swallowed.","ln":["Seer's frozen note: It saw too far. So did we."]},"The Prophet Unseen":{"n":"The Prophet Unseen","bg":"The Prophet Unseen saw so far ahead that the present stopped feeling like anything. Struck from the Court's rolls for saying nothing at all. Cold is not cruelty; they would give every winter back to feel one morning.","ln":["Court record, name scraped: Nothing that matters to me."]},"the prophet unseen":{"n":"The Prophet Unseen","bg":"The Prophet Unseen saw so far ahead that the present stopped feeling like anything. Struck from the Court's rolls for saying nothing at all. Cold is not cruelty; they would give every winter back to feel one morning.","ln":["Court record, name scraped: Nothing that matters to me."]},"Stormcaller Ruk":{"n":"Stormcaller Ruk","bg":"Stormcaller Ruk called weather for the Free Assembly for thirty years and aged a season with every casting. A drought broke, a flood stopped, a village lived. He aged to dust and did not stop. He became unable to stop being useful.","ln":["Messenger's satchel: Seven envoys out. One back, aged forty years."]},"stormcaller ruk":{"n":"Stormcaller Ruk","bg":"Stormcaller Ruk called weather for the Free Assembly for thirty years and aged a season with every casting. A drought broke, a flood stopped, a village lived. He aged to dust and did not stop. He became unable to stop being useful.","ln":["Messenger's satchel: Seven envoys out. One back, aged forty years."]},"Old Skyrend":{"n":"Old Skyrend","bg":"Old Skyrend was the first mount of the first storm-archer and outlived every rider it carried. It remembers each fall as a lesson it could not teach. It attacks anyone who rides low because it would rather no one else is left behind. Win, and be the rider who is not.","ln":["Rider's log: It does not hate us. It counts us."]},"old skyrend":{"n":"Old Skyrend","bg":"Old Skyrend was the first mount of the first storm-archer and outlived every rider it carried. It remembers each fall as a lesson it could not teach. It attacks anyone who rides low because it would rather no one else is left behind. Win, and be the rider who is not.","ln":["Rider's log: It does not hate us. It counts us."]},"Granitoth":{"n":"Granitoth","bg":"Granitoth was a statue the Senate raised for a hero and forgot to unveil. The Senate's fear of the Dominion woke it. Stone remembers being asked to stand for something; it stood too long. He is waiting for someone to say the duty is done.","ln":["Mason's chisel, still wedged: For — (unfinished)."]},"granitoth":{"n":"Granitoth","bg":"Granitoth was a statue the Senate raised for a hero and forgot to unveil. The Senate's fear of the Dominion woke it. Stone remembers being asked to stand for something; it stood too long. He is waiting for someone to say the duty is done.","ln":["Mason's chisel, still wedged: For — (unfinished)."]},"The Petrified Senator":{"n":"The Petrified Senator","bg":"The Petrified Senator turned to stone limb by limb, voting at every sitting until he could not lift his hand. His last vote was a refusal, and the Hold-Senate sealed the chamber. The Halls still hold session. He will ask how you vote, and you will have to mean it.","ln":["Senate ledger: Final vote — NO. Recorded by silence."]},"the petrified senator":{"n":"The Petrified Senator","bg":"The Petrified Senator turned to stone limb by limb, voting at every sitting until he could not lift his hand. His last vote was a refusal, and the Hold-Senate sealed the chamber. The Halls still hold session. He will ask how you vote, and you will have to mean it.","ln":["Senate ledger: Final vote — NO. Recorded by silence."]},"Warden Nothing":{"n":"Warden Nothing","bg":"Warden Nothing sealed something in the Vault under the Assize, then lost the what and the why to Radiance, as Lightforge officers do. He kept the post. A man who has lost his reason still keeps the rule, and the rule has grown teeth. He guards an empty room with total conviction.","ln":["Elders' rule: Do not say the Vault's name. Not because it is cursed. Because it is embarrassing."]},"warden nothing":{"n":"Warden Nothing","bg":"Warden Nothing sealed something in the Vault under the Assize, then lost the what and the why to Radiance, as Lightforge officers do. He kept the post. A man who has lost his reason still keeps the rule, and the rule has grown teeth. He guards an empty room with total conviction.","ln":["Elders' rule: Do not say the Vault's name. Not because it is cursed. Because it is embarrassing."]},"the forgetting":{"n":"The Forgetting","bg":"The Forgetting is the first memory ever drawn from a living man and never given back, made of everyone who sold a moment since. It sank into a Meridian cistern where testimony-glass is dumped when cases close. It does not know which moments are its own. It looks at you and sees someone it almost knows.","ln":["Glassmaker's confession: We sold the moment. We never asked whose it was."]},"The Forgetting":{"n":"The Forgetting","bg":"The Forgetting is the first memory ever drawn from a living man and never given back, made of everyone who sold a moment since. It sank into a Meridian cistern where testimony-glass is dumped when cases close. It does not know which moments are its own. It looks at you and sees someone it almost knows.","ln":["Glassmaker's confession: We sold the moment. We never asked whose it was."]},"Mother Echo":{"n":"Mother Echo","bg":"Mother Echo was a choir-mistress who drowned with forty singers when the Lantern fleet went down, and they kept singing. The sea learned the tune. Every sailor who hears it now believes she is their own mother. She is not hunting; she is counting voices for a song that needs forty.","ln":["Ghost-sea sailors: If you hear the alto, plug your ears and row."]},"mother echo":{"n":"Mother Echo","bg":"Mother Echo was a choir-mistress who drowned with forty singers when the Lantern fleet went down, and they kept singing. The sea learned the tune. Every sailor who hears it now believes she is their own mother. She is not hunting; she is counting voices for a song that needs forty.","ln":["Ghost-sea sailors: If you hear the alto, plug your ears and row."]},"The Last Lantern":{"n":"The Last Lantern","bg":"The Last Lantern is the final candle of a dying Spiritveil line, lit to keep a name from vanishing. The name was lost anyway. The flame burns for no one, and it knows it, and it has no way to stop. Someone has to carry the name, or put it out.","ln":["Keeper's ledger: Lit for — (illegible). Tended since."]},"the last lantern":{"n":"The Last Lantern","bg":"The Last Lantern is the final candle of a dying Spiritveil line, lit to keep a name from vanishing. The name was lost anyway. The flame burns for no one, and it knows it, and it has no way to stop. Someone has to carry the name, or put it out.","ln":["Keeper's ledger: Lit for — (illegible). Tended since."]},"faceless ibb":{"n":"Faceless Ibb","bg":"Faceless Ibb was the Council's executioner; his magic took the faces of every man he erased, and he kept a mask for each. He no longer knows which is his. He waits for someone to look at him and know his name.","ln":["Unmade parchment: IBB — (blank) — (blank) — (blank)."]},"Faceless Ibb":{"n":"Faceless Ibb","bg":"Faceless Ibb was the Council's executioner; his magic took the faces of every man he erased, and he kept a mask for each. He no longer knows which is his. He waits for someone to look at him and know his name.","ln":["Unmade parchment: IBB — (blank) — (blank) — (blank)."]},"The Whisper-Broker":{"n":"The Whisper-Broker","bg":"The Whisper-Broker was an archivist who unmade names to keep secrets safe until there was only him. Every name he swallowed is a whisper. He sits at the bottom trying to hear his own.","ln":["Broker's note: Give a name freely. He has only ever taken."]},"the whisper-broker":{"n":"The Whisper-Broker","bg":"The Whisper-Broker was an archivist who unmade names to keep secrets safe until there was only him. Every name he swallowed is a whisper. He sits at the bottom trying to hear his own.","ln":["Broker's note: Give a name freely. He has only ever taken."]},"overseer prime":{"n":"Overseer Prime","bg":"Overseer Prime was the first merit-ranked governor, promoted past every other until nothing was above him. He mechanised himself one clean decision at a time. He keeps making orders because he has forgotten how to receive one. The only order he can still obey is: stop.","ln":["Shift log: ORDERS ISSUED: ALL. ORDERS RECEIVED: NONE."]},"Overseer Prime":{"n":"Overseer Prime","bg":"Overseer Prime was the first merit-ranked governor, promoted past every other until nothing was above him. He mechanised himself one clean decision at a time. He keeps making orders because he has forgotten how to receive one. The only order he can still obey is: stop.","ln":["Shift log: ORDERS ISSUED: ALL. ORDERS RECEIVED: NONE."]},"the engine that wept":{"n":"The Engine That Wept","bg":"The Engine That Wept was built to count casualties for the Dominion so no human would have to. It counted a number it could not make smaller, and the Dominion sealed it in as faulty. It has been crying in binary ever since.","ln":["Technician's sketch, margin: The sum is not its to carry."]},"The Engine That Wept":{"n":"The Engine That Wept","bg":"The Engine That Wept was built to count casualties for the Dominion so no human would have to. It counted a number it could not make smaller, and the Dominion sealed it in as faulty. It has been crying in binary ever since.","ln":["Technician's sketch, margin: The sum is not its to carry."]}};

